tel 1.0

base jacinta
import soundness

info   JSON as a typed, immutable value: parsed, built, navigated, converted to Scala types and back, and streamed out
detail
    _Jacinta_ reads and writes [JSON](https://www.json.org/). Its central type is `Json`, an
    immutable document (or any value within one) which is parsed from text or bytes with
    `read[Json]`, built with `Json.make` or the `j"…"` interpolator, navigated by key and index,
    and converted to a Scala type with `as[T]` or from one with `in[Json]`. The conversions are
    typeclasses — `Json.Encodable` and `Json.Decodable` — which the compiler derives for case
    classes, enumerations and sealed hierarchies from their shape, so a record converts to an
    object and back with nothing written by hand, and a mismatch between the data and the type
    raises a `Json.Error` naming the reason and, where it can, the field.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import strategies.throwUnsafely
    import formatting.compactJsonFormatting

    case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)

    val document: Json = t"""{"name": "Percy", "age": 30}""".read[Json]
    document(t"name").as[Text]                  // t"Percy"
    document.as[Person]                         // Person(t"Percy", 30)
    Person(t"Hal", 40).in[Json].show            // t"""{"name":"Hal","age":40}"""
    t"""{"name": "Poins", "age": 25}""".read[Person in Json]
    ```

    A document need never be built at all: a type with a `Json.Parsable` is read *directly* from
    the token stream (`Json.Parsable.derived`, `Json.Parsable.staged`, `Inlinable.parsable`, or
    `derives Json.Direct` for a generated parser and printer), and a type with a `Json.Printable`
    is written straight to text or bytes through `in[Text over Json]` and `in[Data over Json]`.
    The `codecs.direct`, `codecs.derived` and `codecs.strict` imports choose between the generated
    and the tree-based paths for every record in scope. Direct parsing and printing bring the
    library within a small margin of the fastest JVM JSON libraries while keeping every value
    typed.

    Large documents stream: `Json.emit` serializes on a fiber and hands out text as it is
    produced, the push form `Json.emit[Text]`/`Json.emit[Data]` delivers blocks on the caller's
    thread, and `Json.lend` lends the writer's own buffers without a copy. Parsing is streaming
    too: a `Json` is `Aggregable by Data`, so it reads from any source turbulence can stream.

    Beyond conversion, the library offers `JsonPointer` (RFC 6901 paths, written with `jp"…"`),
    source positions for every value when `parsing.trackPositions` is in scope (`locate` and
    `locateKey`), dynamic field access (`json.name`) licensed by `dynamicAccess.dynamicJson` or a
    `dynamically[Json]` block, lenses and optics over documents (`json.lens(_.a.b = …)`, with
    `Each` and `Filter` from the `optics` package), a `NumberMode` deciding what becomes of a
    number too long for a `Long` or `Double`, and `Json.Provider`, which reads a JSON Schema
    document as the code compiles and yields typed records from matching JSON.

    The library is split into six source sets:

    - `jacinta.core` — `Json` and everything above; `JsonPointer`, `NumberMode`, `Inlinable`,
      the `j"…"` and `jp"…"` interpolators, and the choice packages `formatting`,
      `numberModes`, `optionalityOptions`, `discriminables`, `dynamicAccess` and `conversions`.
      Compiles for the JVM, Scala.js and Scala Native.
    - `jacinta.schema` — `JsonSchema`, a JSON Schema document derived from a Scala type or read
      from JSON, `verify`, which checks a `Json` against the schema of a type and returns it
      typed so that its fields navigate with compile-time checking, and the `@memo` annotation.
    - `jacinta.optics` — the `optics` package: `jsonLens`, which makes `json.lens(…)` work, and
      the `Each`, `Filter` and ordinal optics over arrays.
    - `jacinta.http` — JVM only: the `postables`, `servables` and `construables` packages, which
      send a `Json` as an `application/json` request body, serve one as a response, and read an
      API's JSON body as a `Json`.
    - `jacinta.time` — the `encodables`, `decodables` and `parsables` packages, which carry
      aviation's `Instant` and `Duration` as whole numbers of milliseconds.
    - `jacinta.staged` — `InlinableStager`, the compile-time machinery which lets a user-written
      `Inlinable` be evaluated as a generated parser is spliced; nothing in it is called by hand.

    Everything is exported to the `soundness` umbrella, and every choice package here merges
    with the umbrella's package of the same name. The choice packages hold *givens*, and
    `import soundness.*` does not import givens, so each is imported by name:
    `import formatting.indentedJsonFormatting`, `import numberModes.doubleNumberMode`,
    `import discriminables.jsonByKindDiscriminable`, `import dynamicAccess.dynamicJson`, and so
    on. The `Json.Formatting` is the one given most code must choose, since `show`, `emit` and
    the HTTP givens all need one.

refer jacinta.Json
refer jacinta.JsonPointer
refer jacinta.JsonSchema
refer jacinta.Inlinable
refer jacinta.NumberMode

entry .Json   an immutable JSON value, and the companion which parses, builds, converts and streams it
  detail
      A `Json` is any JSON value — an object, array, string, number, boolean or `null` — or an
      *absent* value, which is what reaching for a key an object lacks yields. It is immutable:
      every update returns a new `Json`. Two documents are equal when their values are, whatever
      the order of their keys, and a whole number equals the same value written with a decimal
      point.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import formatting.compactJsonFormatting

      val data: Json = t"""{"a": {"b": [10, 20, 30]}, "c": true}""".read[Json]
      data(t"a")(t"b")(1).as[Int]               // 20
      data(t"c").as[Boolean]                    // true
      data(t"missing").as[Optional[Int]]        // Unset
      data.show                                 // t"""{"a":{"b":[10,20,30]},"c":true}"""
      t"""{"x": 1, "y": 2}""".read[Json] == t"""{"y": 2, "x": 1}""".read[Json]   // true
      ```

      **Reading.** A `Json` is `Aggregable by Data` (`.aggregable`), so any source turbulence can
      stream — `Text`, `Data`, a file, an HTTP body — is `read[Json]`, raising `Parse.Error` on
      malformed input through a `Tactic[Parse.Error]`. With `parsing.trackPositions` in scope the
      parser also records every value's line and column, read back with `locate` and `locateKey`
      (`.positionable`). `read[T in Json]` reads a value of type `T` from JSON text in one step,
      directly from the tokens when `T` has a `Json.Parsable`, and through a tree otherwise.

      **Converting.** `as[T]` decodes through a `Json.Decodable` (`#as`), and `value.in[Json]`
      encodes through a `Json.Encodable`. The companion holds the instances for the primitives,
      `Text`, `Unit` (a JSON `null`), `Optional`, `Option`, `List`, `Set`, `Sequence`, `Map` and
      `Json` itself, and derives one for any case class, enumeration or sealed hierarchy
      (`.decodable`, `.encodable`). A conversion that cannot be made raises `Json.Error`, and
      under an accruing strategy every failed field is reported, each with a `Json.Focus` naming
      its JSON pointer.

      **Building.** `Json.make(a = …, b = …)` builds an object from named `Json` arguments
      (`.applyDynamicNamed`), `Json(value)` wraps a primitive (`.apply`), and the `j"…"`
      interpolator writes JSON literally, checked as the code compiles, with holes for encoded
      values, `*`-spread collections and merged maps (`.interpolator`); the same interpolator is
      a pattern (`.extrapolator`).

      **Navigating and updating.** `json(t"key")` and `json(index)` select a field or an element
      (`#apply`). With `dynamicAccess.dynamicJson` imported, a field reads as a member,
      `json.name`, and is set with `json.name = value` or removed with `json.name = Unset`
      (`#selectDynamic`, `#updateDynamic`); a document `verify`'d against a type navigates its
      fields with compile-time checking and needs no such import. Deeper updates are written
      with `json.lens(_.a.b = …)` from the `optics` package.

      **Writing.** `show` renders the whole document as `Text` in the `Json.Formatting` in scope
      (`.showable`; `formatting.compactJsonFormatting` or `formatting.indentedJsonFormatting`),
      `.emit` streams it, and `.lend` lends the writer's buffers. A `Json` is also `Streamable by
      Text`, `Encodable in Text`, an HTTP body (`.abstractable`) and `Inspectable`.

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label A document, as parsed
        code
            import soundness.*
            import strategies.throwUnsafely

            val person: Json = t"""{"name": "Hal", "age": 40, "friends": ["Poins", "Peto"]}""".read[Json]
      frame
        label A field reached by key
        code
            person(t"friends")(0).as[Text]   // t"Poins"
      frame
        label The same field, with dynamic access enabled
        code
            import dynamicAccess.dynamicJson

            person.friends(0).as[Text]   // t"Poins"
      frame
        label The whole document, decoded to a case class
        code
            case class Person(name: Text, age: Int, friends: List[Text])

            person.as[Person]   // Person(t"Hal", 40, List(t"Poins", t"Peto"))
      frame
        label An update, as a new document
        code
            (person.age = 41).as[Person].age   // 41
      ##
      ```

      The companion is assembled from a chain of mixins (`Json1` to `Json4`) which order its
      givens by priority: direct parsers and printers above derived codecs, element-wise
      instances above the universal fallback. Their members are documented under those mixins'
      entries; `object Json` inherits them. The tokenizer behind every read is the top-level `Parser`, which has its own page.
  refer jacinta.Json.Encodable
  refer jacinta.Json.Decodable
  refer jacinta.Json.Parsable
  refer jacinta.Json.Printable
  refer jacinta.Json.Provider
  refer jacinta.Json.Error
  refer jacinta.JsonPointer
  refer jacinta.j
  entry .apply   wraps a primitive value as a `Json`: `Json(42)`, `Json(t"text")`, `Json(true)`
      `Json(value)` makes a `Json` from a raw value. The overloads for `Int`, `Short`, `Byte`,
      `Long`, `Float` and `Double` store the number in the tree's own forms (an integer as a
      `Long`, a `Float` as a `Double`), since a raw `Int` in the tree would be read as a packed
      small decimal, not an integer. `apply(value: Any)` takes anything else as it is, so it is
      for values already in the tree's representation (a `String`, a `Boolean`, `Json.JsonNull`)
      rather than arbitrary Scala values, which should go through `in[Json]` instead. The
      two-argument form, `Json(value, positions)`, attaches a `Json.PositionIndex` to a
      value, as the tracking parser does.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      Json(42)        // a JSON number
      Json(t"hello")  // a JSON string
      Json(true)      // a JSON boolean
      ```
  entry .ast   wraps a `Json.Ast` node as a `Json`
      The inverse of `.unseal`: `Json.ast(node)` is the document whose root is the given tree
      node. `Json.ast(Json.Ast(Unset))` is the absent value a missing key yields.
  entry .applyDynamicNamed   `Json.make(key = json, …)`: an object built from named `Json` arguments
      `Json` is `Dynamic`, and its only dynamic method is `make`: `Json.make(a = 1.in[Json],
      b = t"two".in[Json])` builds the object `{"a":1,"b":"two"}`, keeping the arguments' order.
      Each argument must already be a `Json`; with `conversions.encodableToJson` imported, any
      value `Encodable in Json` may be passed directly.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import formatting.compactJsonFormatting

      Json.make(a = 1.in[Json], b = t"two".in[Json], c = true.in[Json]).show
      // t"""{"a":1,"b":"two","c":true}"""
      Json.make(outer = Json.make(inner = 1.in[Json])).show   // t"""{"outer":{"inner":1}}"""
      ```
  entry .unseal   the `Json.Ast` tree beneath a `Json`, for inspecting its kind directly
      Breaking through the `Json` abstraction is a deliberate, named action: `Json.unseal(json)`
      is the root `Json.Ast` node, on which the `Json.Ast` predicates (`isLong`, `isDouble`,
      `isNumber`, `isString`, `isBoolean`, `isNull`, `isObject`, `isArray`, `isAbsent`) and
      `primitive` answer what the value is.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      Json.unseal(t"42".read[Json]).isLong           // true
      Json.unseal(t"3.14".read[Json]).isNumber       // true
      Json.unseal(t""""x"""".read[Json]).primitive   // Json.Primitive.String
      ```
  entry .emit   streams a document's text: pulled from a fiber, or pushed to a function block by block
      Two forms. `Json.emit(json)` serializes on a fiber and returns an `Iterator[Text]` which
      hands out the text as it is produced, so a large document can be written to a socket or a
      file before it is fully rendered; it needs a `Json.Formatting`, a `Monitor` and a
      `Probate`, so it runs under `supervise`. The push form, `Json.emit[medium](json, deliver)`,
      serializes on the caller's thread with no fiber and hands each block to `deliver` as it
      fills: `emit[Text]` delivers text through a `Producer[Text]`, and `emit[Data]` delivers
      UTF-8 bytes written directly by the byte-level serializer, which escapes and encodes each
      string in one pass and renders a BCD number from its nibbles with no `String` round trip.
      The medium is a `Json.Emitter`, and the type argument is needed when `deliver` is an
      untyped lambda. The push form needs a `Json.Formatting` and a `Buffering`, whose capacity
      sizes the blocks.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      Json.emit[Data](json, chunk => socket.write(chunk))

      supervise:
        Json.emit(json).each(chunk => out.write(chunk))
      ```

      Every form renders the same text as `show`.
  entry .lend   the borrowing form of the push `emit`: each UTF-8 block is lent, not copied
      `Json.lend(json)(lending)` serializes to UTF-8 on the caller's thread and lends each filled
      block to `lending` as a `Region[Data]` with a branded `Interval` proving every index in
      range, valid only for the duration of the call — the discipline of `Stream.lend` — so
      nothing is copied. A consumer which must keep the bytes materializes them itself;
      `Json.emit[Data]` is that consumer for the common case. Needs a `Json.Formatting` and a
      `Buffering`.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      Json.lend(json): region =>
        interval => region.visit(interval)(index => sink.put(region(index)))
      ```
  entry .Emitter   how the push form of `Json.emit` reaches its consumer: as text or as UTF-8 bytes
    detail
        A `Json.Emitter[medium]` is the typeclass `Json.emit[medium](json, deliver)` dispatches
        on. The two instances are in the companion: `.text` for `Text` blocks and `.data` for
        `Data` blocks. A medium is chosen by the type argument, `Json.emit[Data](…)`.
    entry .text   delivers text blocks, through a `Producer[Text]`
    entry .data   delivers UTF-8 blocks, materialized from the byte-level writer's lent regions
    entry #emit   serializes a document and hands each block to `deliver`, in the `Buffering`'s capacity
  entry .Formatting   how a document renders: the indentation unit, if any, and whether a newline ends it
    detail
        A `Json.Formatting` is needed by `show`, `emit`, `lend`, the `Streamable` and `Encodable
        in Text` givens, `in[Text over Json]` and the HTTP givens. `#indent` is the whitespace
        unit per nesting level, or `Unset` for minimal single-line JSON, and `#trailingNewline`
        appends a final newline. The two standard choices are givens in the `formatting`
        package, imported by name: `formatting.compactJsonFormatting` (no whitespace at all)
        and `formatting.indentedJsonFormatting` (two spaces per level). It is a
        `zephyrine.Formatting`, so a generic serializer can take one.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        given Json.Formatting = Json.Formatting(t"    ", trailingNewline = true)
        ```
    entry .apply   a formatting with the given indentation unit and trailing-newline choice
    entry #indent   the whitespace unit per nesting level, or `Unset` for single-line output
    entry #trailingNewline   whether a newline follows the closing character
  entry .JsonString   the tree's representation of a JSON string: a `String`
  entry .JsonNumber   the tree's representations of a JSON number: `Long`, `Double`, `Bcd` or a packed `Int`
      A number up to about fifteen digits parses to a `Long` or a `Double`; a longer one becomes
      a `Bcd` (every digit kept) under the default `NumberMode`. The `Int` form is a *packed*
      small binary-coded decimal of up to seven nibbles, not an integer, which is why
      `Json(int)` widens an `Int` to a `Long` rather than storing it raw.
  entry .JsonBoolean   the tree's representation of a JSON boolean: a `Boolean`
  entry .JsonNull   the sentinel which represents a JSON `null` in the tree, and its type
      A distinct object rather than the JVM `null`, so that an explicit `null` in a document
      stays disjoint from an *absent* value (`Unset`), which is what a missing key yields;
      `Json.Ast.isNull` tests for it.
  entry .JsonObject   the tree's representation of an object: a frozen `Array[Any]` of alternating keys and values
  entry .JsonArray   the tree's representations of an array: a frozen `Array[Any]`, `Array[Long]` or `Array[Int]`
      A heterogeneous array is an `Array[Any]`, padded to odd length so that it is
      distinguishable from an object (always even); an array of numbers alone is stored unboxed
      as packed BCD `Long`s or `Int`s.
  entry .aggregable   reads a `Json` from a stream of `Data`: the given behind `source.read[Json]`
      The `Aggregable by Data` instance which parses any streamed source — `Text`, `Data`, a
      file, a response body — into a `Json`. It needs a `Tactic[Parse.Error]`, raised on
      malformed input with the line and column of the fault, and a `PositionTracking`: with
      `parsing.trackPositions` in scope the positions of every value are recorded for `locate`;
      the default records nothing and pays nothing. A single in-memory block is parsed in
      place; a stream is parsed as it arrives.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      t"""[1, 2, 3]""".read[Json]
      ```
  entry .showable   renders a document as `Text` in the `Json.Formatting` in scope
      `json.show` is the single route to a document's text: compact under
      `formatting.compactJsonFormatting`, indented under `formatting.indentedJsonFormatting`.
      Numbers are written from the digits the parser saw, so a document round-trips without
      losing precision; strings are escaped per RFC 8259.
  entry .streamable   a document as a `Stream[Text]`, serialized on a fiber as it is consumed
      The `Streamable by Text over Credit` instance built on `.emit`'s pull form, so it needs a
      `Json.Formatting`, a `Monitor` and a `Probate` (a `supervise` block).
  entry .inspectable   a debug rendering: the compact text, whatever formatting is in scope
      An inspection is single-line by convention, so this fixes the compact formatting rather
      than taking one from the call site, and needs no given at all.
  entry .abstractable   a document as an HTTP body, `application/json` in the `Codepage` in scope
      The `Abstractable across HttpStreams` instance: the content type is `application/json;
      charset=…` from the `Codepage` given, and the body is `show` encoded in it, so it needs
      a `Json.Formatting` too. The `postables` and `servables` packages of `jacinta.http` are
      the explicit request and response givens.
  entry .decodable   `Json` from text: reads a document from `Text`, raising `Parse.Error`
      `Json is Decodable in Text` reads a document from `Text` (`t"…".as[Json]`, or a `Json`
      field inside a record decoded from another format), raising `Parse.Error`; it is also
      what superlunary's staged `Json` values are pickled through.
  entry .instantiable   a `Json` from HTTP request text, raising `Parse.Error`
      The `Instantiable across HttpRequests from Text` instance, by which a request body typed
      as JSON is parsed; it captures the `Tactic[Parse.Error]` in scope.
  entry .positionable   `json.locate(pointer)` and `json.locateKey(pointer)`: where a value sat in the source
      The `Positionable by JsonPointer to Json.Ast.Position` instance. `locate` is the line and
      column (and offset and length) of the value a pointer names, spanning the whole value —
      a negative number includes its sign, an object runs from its opening brace to its closing
      one — and `locateKey` is the position of the *key* matching the pointer's final segment,
      for underlining a misspelled field name. Both are `Unset` when the pointer does not
      resolve (a missing key, an index past the end), and always `Unset` for a document parsed
      without `parsing.trackPositions` in scope.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import parsing.trackPositions

      val tracked = t"{\n  \"a\": 42\n}".read[Json]
      tracked.locate(JsonPointer()(t"a")).let(_.line)      // 2
      tracked.locateKey(JsonPointer()(t"a")).let(_.column) // 3
      ```
  entry .interpolator   the macro behind `j"…"`: a JSON literal checked as it compiles, with typed holes
      The `Interpolable` instance for `Json`. The literal is parsed at compile time, and a
      syntax error is reported at the offending character. A hole `$x` substitutes any value
      `Encodable in Json`; `$xs*` inside an array splices every element of a collection
      (an `Iterable`, or a branded `List(…)` literal); a `$map` hole among an object's fields
      merges a `Map[Text, Json]` into the object. See `jacinta.j`.
  entry .extrapolator   the macro behind `case j"…" =>`: a JSON pattern binding its holes
      The `Extrapolable` instance for `Json`. In a `match`, `case j"""{"a": $a}"""` matches an
      object with the literal keys and binds each hole to the `Json` found there; an array
      pattern matches by length, and a trailing `$rest*` binds the remaining elements as a
      `Json` array. A document of another shape declines the case.
  entry .jsonEncodableInText   a document as compact `Text`, whatever formatting is in scope
      The `Encodable in Text` instance, `json.encode`: always compact, so a `Json` field
      written inside another format is single-line.
  entry .discriminatedUnion   a `Discriminable` which tags a variant with a field of the given name
      `Json.discriminatedUnion[Shape](t"type")` is a `Json.DiscriminantField` under the key
      `type`: the shape `{"radius":1.0,"type":"Circle"}`. The `discriminables` package's
      `jsonByTypeDiscriminable` and `jsonByKindDiscriminable` are this for `type` and `kind`.
  entry .Encodable   the typeclass which turns a value into a `Json`, carrying the shape it produces
    detail
        `T is Json.Encodable` is an `Encodable in Json` which also knows its `Morphology` — the
        format-neutral shape of what it writes — so that a JSON Schema derived from a type
        (`JsonSchema.derived`, `verify`) is coherent with the codec by construction. It is what
        `value.in[Json]` resolves. The companion holds instances for the primitives, `Text`,
        `Unit`, `Ordinal`, `Optional`, `Option`, `List`, `Set`, `Sequence`, `Map` and `Json`,
        and derives one for any case class, enumeration or sealed hierarchy (`.encodable`); a
        type with an `Encodable in Text` encodes as a JSON string.

        A custom instance is built with `.apply`, giving the shape as a thunk and the encoding
        as a lambda, and belongs in the type's companion:

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import formatting.compactJsonFormatting

        case class Celsius(degrees: Double)

        object Celsius:
          given Celsius is Json.Encodable =
            Json.Encodable(() => Morphology.Str): celsius => Json(t"${celsius.degrees}°C")

        Celsius(21.5).in[Json].show   // t""""21.5°C""""
        ```

        A derived encoder uses, for each field, the instance in scope for the field's type; to
        override one field alone, a `Specific over Json.Encodable` names the path.
    refer jacinta.Json.Decodable
    refer jacinta.Json.Printable
    entry .apply   an encoder from a shape thunk and an encoding lambda
        `Json.Encodable(() => shape)(value => json)`. The shape is an explicit thunk, so a
        recursive derivation can defer it; `Morphology.Any` is the honest shape for anything
        the schema machinery should not constrain.
    entry #shape   the `Morphology` of what this encoder writes
    entry #Form   the format this encoder targets: `Json`
  entry .Decodable   the typeclass which reads a value from a `Json`, carrying the shape it expects
    detail
        `T is Json.Decodable` is a `Decodable in Json` which also knows its `Morphology`, so
        `verify` and the schema machinery check exactly what the decoder reads. It is what
        `json.as[T]` and `read[T in Json]` resolve (the latter through the tree, unless `T` also
        has a `Json.Parsable`). The companion holds instances for the primitives, `Text`,
        `Unit` (which accepts only `null`), `Ordinal`, `Bytes`, `Optional`, `Option`, `List`,
        `Set`, `Sequence`, `Map` and `Json`, and derives one for any case class, enumeration or
        sealed hierarchy (`.decodable`); a type with a `Decodable in Text` is decoded from a
        JSON string through it.

        Every instance raises `Json.Error` through the `Tactic[Json.Error]` in scope: a value
        of the wrong kind is `NotType`, a required field the object lacks is `Absent`, and an
        array index past the end is `OutOfRange`. Under an accruing strategy every failing
        field is reported with its `Json.Focus`.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        case class Celsius(degrees: Double)

        object Celsius:
          given (Celsius is Json.Decodable) =
            Json.Decodable(Morphology.Real): json => Celsius(json.as[Double])

        t"21.5".read[Json].as[Celsius]   // Celsius(21.5)
        ```

        A case class with both a `Reflection` and a hand-written `Json.Decodable` is read
        *directly* through derivation when `read[T in Json]` is used, diverging from the custom
        decoder; the one-line remedy is `given T is Json.Parsable =
        Json.Parsable.fromDecodable(…)` beside it.
    refer jacinta.Json.Encodable
    refer jacinta.Json.Parsable
    refer jacinta.Json.Error
    entry .apply   a decoder from a shape and a decoding function
        `Json.Decodable(shape)(json => value)`, where the decoding function may be any
        `Decodable in Json` or a lambda over the `Json`. The lambda raises `Json.Error` through
        the tactic it was resolved with.
    entry #shape   the `Morphology` of what this decoder reads
  entry .boolean   decodes a JSON boolean to `Boolean`
      Raises `Json.Error(NotType(…, Boolean))` for any other value. Each primitive decoder
      captures the `Tactic[Json.Error]` it was resolved with.
  entry .double   decodes a JSON number to `Double`, from any numeric form
  entry .float   decodes a JSON number to `Float`
  entry .long   decodes a JSON number to `Long`; a decimal is truncated
  entry .int   decodes a JSON number to `Int`, through `Long`
  entry .ordinalDecodable   decodes a JSON number to an `Ordinal`, counting from zero
  entry .text   decodes a JSON string to `Text`
      Raises `Json.Error(NotType(…, String))` for any other value: a number is not quietly
      rendered as text.
  entry .string   decodes a JSON string to `String`
  entry .unit   decodes a JSON `null` to `()`, and nothing else
      A value which is not `null` raises `NotType(found, Null)`, and an absent value `Absent`.
  entry .jsonDecodable   a `Json` decodes to itself: `json.as[Json]`
      Typed as the plain `Decodable in Json` so that it beats distillate's generic identity
      instance; `Json` nested in a collection or `Optional` goes through `.decodable` instead.
  entry .option   decodes an `Option[T]` field, as `.optional` does, yielding `None`
      The same three `optionalityOptions` policies, with `None` for an absent key or a `null`
      and `Some` otherwise.
  entry .optionEncodable   encodes an `Option[T]`: `None` encodes as absent, so an object omits the field
  entry .integralEncodable   encodes any `Integral` type as a JSON integer
  entry .textEncodableInJson   encodes `Text` as a JSON string
  entry .stringEncodable   encodes `String` as a JSON string
  entry .doubleEncodable   encodes `Double` as a JSON number
  entry .intEncodable   encodes `Int` as a JSON integer
  entry .longEncodable   encodes `Long` as a JSON integer
  entry .booleanEncodable   encodes `Boolean` as a JSON boolean
  entry .unitEncodable   encodes `Unit` as a JSON `null`
  entry .ordinalEncodable   encodes an `Ordinal` as its zero-based JSON integer
  entry .jsonEncodable   a `Json` encodes as itself
  entry .listEncodable   encodes a `List[T]` as a JSON array of its elements
      Needs a `T is Json.Encodable`, taken by name so that a recursive type derives.
  entry .setEncodable   encodes a `Set[T]` as a JSON array of its elements, in the set's order
  entry .seriesEncodable   encodes a `Sequence[T]` as a JSON array of its elements
  entry .mapEncodable   encodes a `Map[K, V]` as a JSON object, each key encoded as text
      The key type needs an `Encodable in Text`, and the value a `Json.Encodable`.
  entry .array   decodes a JSON array into any `Iterable` collection, with a `Factory` in scope
      Each element is decoded at its own `Json.Focus` (`#/items/3`), so under an accruing
      strategy every bad element is reported. Needs a `Tactic[Json.Error]` and a `Foci[Json.Focus]`.
  entry .listDecodable   decodes a JSON array into a `List[T]`
  entry .setDecodable   decodes a JSON array into a `Set[T]`
  entry .seriesDecodable   decodes a JSON array into a `Sequence[T]`
  entry .map   decodes a JSON object into a `Map[K, V]`, each key decoded from text
      The key type needs a `Decodable in Text`; `Map[Text, Int]` reads `{"a": 1, "b": 2}`.
  entry .Parsable   the typeclass which reads a value straight from JSON tokens, with no tree built
    detail
        `T is Json.Parsable` consumes tokens from a `Json.Reader` instead of walking a `Json`,
        so `read[T in Json]` (and `read[T in Json]` over any stream) instantiates `T` without
        materializing a tree, saving the allocation, boxing and second traversal. It is the
        opt-in surface of direct parsing: a type parses directly once it has an instance,
        through `.derived`, `.staged`, `Inlinable.parsable`, `derives Json.Direct` or
        `import codecs.direct`, and nothing changes for a type without one. The companion holds
        instances for the primitives (`Json.booleanParsable` and its kin) and, for a collection
        whose element has opted in, `Optional`, `Option`, `List`, `Set`, `Sequence`, `Map` and
        any `Iterable`.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)

        object Person:
          given Person is Json.Parsable = Json.Parsable.derived

        t"""{"name": "Hal", "age": 40}""".read[Person in Json]   // read with no tree built
        ```

        A hand-written instance is built with `.apply` from a shape and a function of the
        reader; `.fromDecodable` bridges an existing `Json.Decodable`, which is also how a
        custom decoder is made to win over derivation. The `optionalityOptions` policies apply
        to direct parsers as to decoders, with one limit: a lenient fault is tolerated only
        when the value on the wire is of the wrong *kind*, which the parser can skip whole; a
        value of the right kind which fails within it has already been partly consumed and
        still raises.

        The companion inherits `generated`, the `codecs.direct` given, from `Json.Parsable2`.
    refer jacinta.Json.Reader
    refer jacinta.Json.Field
    refer jacinta.Json.Decodable
    refer jacinta.Inlinable
    entry .apply   a parser from a shape and a function of the `Json.Reader`
        `Json.Parsable(Morphology.Str)(reader => Celsius(reader.string()))`. The reader
        carries its own `Tactic[Parse.Error]`, so the body needs no error vocabulary: a
        malformed or mistyped token aborts through the read call's ambient tactic.
    entry .derived   a wisteria-derived direct parser for a case class or sum, as a one-line opt-in
        `given T is Json.Parsable = Json.Parsable.derived`. Fields are read through the
        `Json.Field` fallback chain, so nested records parse directly too; a sum's
        `Discriminable in Json` chooses the token-level dispatch.
    entry .staged   a monomorphic parser generated at compile time, with no interpretive machinery at runtime
        The staged counterpart of `.derived`, with identical semantics: a case class parses
        through typed local slots and packed-literal key dispatch, and a sealed sum dispatches
        its tag through a comparison chain into per-variant parsers. A staged sum needs a
        field-discriminated `Discriminable` (`jsonByKindDiscriminable` or another
        `DiscriminantField`); other shapes fail at instance construction. Method-local
        classes, curried case classes, singleton variants and generic sums stay on `.derived`.
    entry .fromDecodable   a parser which materializes one value and hands it to a `Json.Decodable`
        The universal bridge from the tree: a field type with only a `Json.Decodable` parses
        through this, and it is the one-line escape hatch when a custom decoder must beat a
        derived direct parser: `given T is Json.Parsable = Json.Parsable.fromDecodable(…)`.
    entry .fromField   a nominal `Json.Parsable` over any `Json.Parsing`
    entry .declared   the parser a type generated with `derives Json.Direct`
        The given which reads a `Json.Direct[T]` in scope — the companion member `derives
        Json.Direct` generates — as `T`'s `Json.Parsable`.
    entry .Direct   the base class of generated parsers
        Generated code receives the reader as a neutral carrier and the capability is asserted
        at this rim; a generated parser implements `parseCarrier`. Not for hand-written code.
    entry .parseField   calls a field's parser from generated code
        A support point for generated parsers, which are spliced into user modules and may
        only reference public members; not for hand-written code.
    entry .absentField   a field's absent value, from generated code
    entry .optionality   an `Optional[T]` parser over a `T` parser, applying the `optionalityOptions` policies
        A wire `null` and an absent key both read as `Unset` unless the `Nullity` or `Absence`
        policy in scope is strict; under lenient `Fault` a value of the wrong kind is skipped
        whole and reads as `Unset`.
    entry .boxed   an `Option[T]` parser over a `T` parser, as `.optionality` yields `None`
    entry .iterable   a parser for any `Iterable` collection over an element parser
        Each element is parsed at its own `Json.Focus` when a `Foci` is active, so every bad
        element accrues; the inert default pays nothing.
    entry .dictionary   a `Map[K, V]` parser over a value parser, decoding each key from text
    entry .discriminantField   the tag field of a field-discriminated sum, for staged parsers
        Panics for a `Discriminable` which is not a `Json.DiscriminantField`, since a staged
        sum has no single scan-ahead step for the other shapes.
    entry .wireKeys   a product's wire keys, with `@name` renames applied
        A support point for staged parsers.
    entry .missing   raises `Json.Error(Absent)` for a required field whose key was not on the wire
    entry .focusing   runs a field read at its `Json.Focus`, compiled away when no `Foci` is active
    entry .assemble   constructs a product positionally from a filled value buffer, through its `Mirror`
    entry .product   the derived product parser's engine: a key-dispatching parse loop over field parsers
        Takes a thunk of `(wire key, parser, declared default)` triples and a constructor over
        the filled buffer, and reads an object by `Json.KeyTable` dispatch, skipping unknown
        keys, filling absent keys from defaults, and accruing every missing required field at
        its focus once the object is fully consumed.
  entry .Parsing   what `Json.Parsable` and `Json.Field` share: a token parser with a shape
    detail
        A `distillate.Parsable` over `Json` with a `Json.Reader`. The two subtraits add nothing
        of their own: they exist so that neither is a subtype of the other, keeping nominal
        instances and the field fallback from competing. `#absent` is what a field of this
        type yields when its key is absent: an error unless overridden (`Unset` for
        `Optional`, `None` for `Option`); a wire `null` never reaches it.
    entry #Transport   the format read: `Json`
    entry #Reader   the reader consumed: `Json.Reader`
    entry #shape   the `Morphology` of what this parser reads
    entry #absent   the value for an absent key: raises `Json.Error(Absent)` unless overridden
  entry .Field   the operational face of direct parsing: how a *field* of a type is read
    detail
        The typeclass the product derivation resolves per field: `Json.Field(parsing)` adapts
        any `Json.Parsing` (`.apply`). Its instances are `.fieldOptional`, `.fieldOption`,
        `.fieldArray`, `.fieldList`, `.fieldSet`, `.fieldSeries` and `.fieldMap` for the
        element-wise cases, and `.field`, the universal fallback, so nested products parse
        directly even where only the outer type opted in. Deliberately not a subtype of
        `Json.Parsable`, and not for use beyond writing a field parser.
    entry .apply   adapts a `Json.Parsing` as a field parser
  entry .booleanParsable   reads a JSON boolean directly
  entry .doubleParsable   reads a JSON number directly as `Double`
  entry .floatParsable   reads a JSON number directly as `Float`
  entry .longParsable   reads a JSON number directly as `Long`
  entry .intParsable   reads a JSON number directly as `Int`
  entry .textParsable   reads a JSON string directly as `Text`
  entry .stringParsable   reads a JSON string directly as `String`
  entry .jsonParsable   reads one whole value as a `Json`, materializing just that value
  entry .optionParsable   an `Option[T]` parses directly when `T` has opted in
  entry .arrayParsable   any `Iterable` collection parses directly when its element has opted in
  entry .listParsable   a `List[T]` parses directly when `T` has opted in
  entry .setParsable   a `Set[T]` parses directly when `T` has opted in
  entry .seriesParsable   a `Sequence[T]` parses directly when `T` has opted in
  entry .mapParsable   a `Map[K, V]` parses directly when `V` has opted in
  entry .aggregableDerived   `import codecs.derived`: read through the tree even where a direct parser exists
  entry .readableDerived   `import codecs.derived`: the whole-`Data` read through the tree
  entry .encodableOverDerived   `import codecs.derived`: write through the tree even where a printer exists
  entry .aggregableStrict   `import codecs.strict`: read directly, or fail to compile
      With `.aggregableStrictFailure`, a pair: a type with a `Json.Parsable` reads directly,
      and a type without one is a compile error naming the type, where `codecs.direct` would
      fall back to the tree.
  entry .encodableOverStrict   `import codecs.strict`: write directly, or fail to compile
  entry .Printable   the typeclass which writes a value's compact JSON text straight to a writer, with no tree
    detail
        The direct-printing counterpart of `Json.Encodable`: `T is Json.Printable` writes `T`
        into a `Json.Ast.Out` — the text writer behind `show` or the byte-level writer behind
        `emit[Data]` — token by token. It is reached through `in[Text over Json]` and `in[Data
        over Json]` whenever the formatting is compact, and its surface is explicit instances,
        `Inlinable.printable[T]`, `derives Json.Direct` and `import codecs.direct`.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import formatting.compactJsonFormatting

        case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)
        given Person is Json.Printable = Inlinable.printable[Person]

        Person(t"Hal", 40).in[Text over Json]   // t"""{"name":"Hal","age":40}""", written directly
        Json.Printable.print(Person(t"Hal", 40))
        ```

        `.print` renders a value whole and `.emit` hands out its text in blocks.

        The companion inherits `generated`, the `codecs.direct` given, from `Json.Printable2`.
    refer jacinta.Json.Encodable
    refer jacinta.Inlinable
