  entry .Parsable2   the mixin holding `Json.Parsable.generated`, the `codecs.direct` parser given
    hidden
    detail
        Not for direct use; see `Json.Parsable`.
    entry #generated   `import codecs.direct`: a generated parser where a record needs one
        Under `Codecs.Direct` a type which `Inlinable.Generates` gets a parser generated at the
        use site, and a type which is not a record falls back to the derived path. Below
        `Json.Parsable.declared`, so a type which derives `Json.Direct` uses the codec it
        generated once.
  entry .Printable2   the mixin holding `Json.Printable.generated`, the `codecs.direct` printer given
    hidden
    detail
        Not for direct use; see `Json.Printable`.
    entry #generated   `import codecs.direct`: a generated printer where a record needs one
        Under `Codecs.Direct` a record gets a printer generated at the use site; a type which is
        not a record takes the derived path.
  entry .Direct   the carrier of `derives Json.Direct`: a type's generated parser and printer, made once
    detail
        `case class Play(…) derives Json.Direct` generates `Play`'s `Json.Parsable` and
        `Json.Printable` once, into its companion, where `Json.Parsable.declared` and
        `Json.Printable.declared` find them with no import. A `Self`-typed typeclass cannot
        appear in a `derives` clause, which is why this parameterised carrier exists.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely
        import formatting.compactJsonFormatting

        case class Point(x: Int, y: Int) derives Json.Direct

        t"""{"x": 1, "y": 2}""".read[Point in Json]   // Point(1, 2), read directly
        Point(1, 2).in[Text over Json]                // t"""{"x":1,"y":2}""", written directly
        ```
    entry .derived   generates the parser and printer, through `Inlinable.parsable` and `Inlinable.printable`
    entry #parsable   the generated `Json.Parsable`
    entry #printable   the generated `Json.Printable`
  entry .KeyTable   a precomputed table of an object's expected keys, for dispatching on them without materializing
    detail
        Built once per record from its wire keys; `Json.Reader#keyIndex(table)` resolves the
        next key against it as the table index, `.Unknown` or `.End`. Keys of up to sixteen
        printable ASCII bytes are compared packed in two `Long`s; others by name. The fastest
        way for a hand-written parser to dispatch on keys.
    entry .apply   a table over the given keys, in index order
    entry .Unknown   the index of a key the table does not hold: `-1`
    entry .End   the index returned once the closing brace is consumed: `-2`
    entry #indexOf   the index of a packed key, by its two words
    entry #indexOfName   the index of a key by its name, or `.Unknown`
  entry .Reader   the restricted rim of the tokenizer which a `Json.Parsable` consumes tokens through
    detail
        Handed to `Json.Parsable#parse`, a `Json.Reader` reads JSON straight off the input, one
        token or structural step per call, skipping leading whitespace. It carries its own
        `Tactic[Parse.Error]`, so a parser body needs no error vocabulary: malformed input and
        mistyped values abort through the read call's ambient tactic, with a `Json.Ast.Issue`
        such as `ExpectedNumber` naming what was expected. It is an exclusive, stateful
        capability, owned by one `parse` call for its duration; nothing of it may be retained.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        case class Point(x: Int, y: Int)

        object Point:
          given Point is Json.Parsable = Json.Parsable(Morphology.Any): reader =>
            var x = 0
            var y = 0
            reader.openObject()
            var key = reader.key()

            while key.present do
              key match
                case t"x" => x = reader.int()
                case t"y" => y = reader.int()
                case _    => reader.skipValue()

              key = reader.key()

            Point(x, y)
        ```

        After `#openObject`, `#key` yields each key in turn and `Unset` once the closing brace
        is consumed; after `#openArray`, `#element` is true while another element follows.
        `#value` materializes one whole value as a `Json` and `#skipValue` skips one, which is
        how unknown keys are passed over. Numbers coerce exactly as the `Json.Ast` accessors
        do, so direct and tree reads yield equal values.
    refer jacinta.Json.Parsable
    refer jacinta.Json.KeyTable
    entry .KeyEnd   the `#keyWord` sentinel once the closing brace is consumed
    entry .KeyOpaque   the `#keyWord` sentinel for a key which cannot be scanned in place
    entry .keyNameOf   the interned name of an opaque key, for generated parsers
    entry #string   consumes a JSON string as `Text`
    entry #boolean   consumes a JSON boolean
    entry #long   consumes a JSON number as `Long`
    entry #int   consumes a JSON number as `Int`
    entry #double   consumes a JSON number as `Double`
    entry #bcd   consumes a JSON number as a `Bcd`, every digit kept
    entry #hasNull   whether the next value is `null`, without consuming it
    entry #primitive   the kind of the next value from its first byte, without consuming it
        `Unset` at the end of input or on a byte that begins no value; the optional wrappers
        compare it against the field's shape before parsing.
    entry #nullValue   consumes a `null`
    entry #openObject   consumes the opening brace of an object
    entry #key   the next key of an open object, or `Unset` once the closing brace is consumed
        Consumes the separator and the colon, leaving the reader at the value.
    entry #keyIndex   the next key resolved against a `Json.KeyTable`, without materializing it
        The table index, `Json.KeyTable.Unknown`, or `Json.KeyTable.End` once the closing brace
        is consumed.
    entry #keyWord   the next key in packed form, for parsers which compare keys against literal constants
        The packed low word (its high word from `#keyWordHigh`), `.KeyEnd`, or `.KeyOpaque`
        when the key cannot be scanned in place, in which case the caller takes the
        `#keyIndex` step instead.
    entry #keyWordFirst   the first key step of the split protocol for generated parsers
    entry #keyWordNext   a subsequent key step of the split protocol for generated parsers
    entry #keyWordHigh   the high word of the key `#keyWord` last scanned
    entry #openArray   consumes the opening bracket of an array
    entry #element   whether another array element follows: true positioned at it, false once the bracket is consumed
    entry #value   consumes one whole value, materialized as a `Json`
    entry #skipValue   consumes one whole value without materializing it
    entry #discriminant   the string value of a key anywhere in the upcoming object, leaving the reader where it was
        The dispatch primitive for a discriminator field which may appear anywhere in the
        object; `Unset` when the object has no such key or its value is not a string.
    entry #fail   aborts through the reader's tactic with a `Json.Ast.Issue`, positioned at the current offset
  entry .Focus   where a decoder is in the document: a JSON pointer, and the source position once known
    detail
        The focus type Jacinta's decoders and parsers track as they descend, so that an error
        raised while reading `person.age` is reported at `#/person/age`. Under an accruing
        strategy the focus of each error is read through `prior`, and `#withPosition` fills in
        the source `Json.Ast.Position` (for a document parsed with `parsing.trackPositions`)
        only when an error is actually rendered, so the success path pays nothing.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)
        case class Contact(person: Person)

        case class Issues(items: List[(Text, Json.Error)] = Nil)(using Diagnostics)
        extends Error(m"${items.size} problems"):
          def +(pointer: Text, error: Json.Error): Issues = Issues(items :+ (pointer, error))

        val damaged = t"""{"person": {"name": 1}}""".read[Json]

        Validate[Issues, [r] =>> r raises Json.Error, Json.Focus]
          ( Issues(),
            { case error: Json.Error => accrual + (prior.let(_.pointer.encode).or(t"#"), error) } )
        . protect(damaged.as[Contact])
        . items.map(_(0))   // List(t"#/person/name", t"#/person/age")
        ```
    entry #pointer   the JSON pointer to the value being read
    entry #position   the value's source position, once `#withPosition` has found it, or `Unset`
    entry #withPosition   the focus with its position looked up in a tracked document
  entry .PositionIndex   the compact record of every value's source position in a tracked document
      An opaque frozen `Array[Int]`, produced by the parser under `parsing.trackPositions` and
      carried by `Json#positionIndex`; `json.locate(pointer)` reads it. Nothing in it is
      addressed by hand.
  entry .Ast   the tree beneath a `Json`: an opaque union of the JSON kinds, with predicates, accessors and parsers
    detail
        `Json.Ast` is the representation a `Json` wraps: a `String`, a number (`Long`,
        `Double`, `Bcd`, or a packed `Int`), a `Boolean`, the `Json.JsonNull` sentinel, an
        object (a frozen `Array[Any]` of alternating keys and values), an array, or `Unset`
        for an absent value. `Json.unseal(json)` exposes a document's root node and
        `Json.ast(node)` wraps one. The extension methods in this companion — the `isX`
        predicates, `.primitive`, and the accessors `.string`, `.long`, `.double`, `.bcd`,
        `.number`, `.boolean`, `.array`, `.obj`, `.arrayLength`, `.arrayElement`,
        `.objectSize`, `.objectKey`, `.objectValue` and `.objectIndexOf` — are in implicit
        scope wherever a `Json.Ast` is used, without entering the umbrella's namespace.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        val node: Json.Ast = Json.unseal(t"""{"a": [1, 2.5, "x"]}""".read[Json])
        node.isObject                                    // true
        node.objectKey(0)                                // "a"
        node.objectValue(0).arrayLength                  // 3
        node.objectValue(0).arrayElement(1).primitive    // Json.Primitive.Number
        node.objectValue(0).arrayElement(1).double       // 2.5
        ```

        The predicates are finer than the primitives: `.isLong` and `.isDouble` distinguish
        the two numeric representations, `.isBcd` and `.isSmallBcd` the two packed ones, and
        `.isNumber` covers all four. The accessors raise `Json.Error(NotType(found, expected))`
        for a node of the wrong kind, and `Absent` for an absent one. `Json.Ast` is also a
        zephyrine `Format`, which gives it the `Position` and `Issue` types every parse error
        carries. The low-level parsers in `.parse` build a tree from bytes; ordinary reading
        goes through `read[Json]`.
    refer jacinta.Json
    refer jacinta.Parser
    entry .name   the format's name, `JSON`
    entry .apply   wraps a raw value already in the tree's representation as an `Ast`
    entry .obj   an object node from parallel arrays of keys and values, or an object node's keys and values
        Two overloads. `Json.Ast.obj(keys, values)` builds an object node from parallel arrays;
        the extension `node.obj` returns an object's keys and values as two parallel arrays,
        raising `Json.Error` for any other kind. The extension materializes two new arrays;
        prefer `.objectKey` and `.objectValue` for a few entries.
    entry .arr   an array node from its elements
    entry .bcdArr   an array of numbers in the packed-`Long` BCD form
    entry .smallBcdArr   an array of numbers in the packed-`Int` BCD form
    entry .arrayPad   the sentinel which pads an even-length array so it is distinguishable from an object
        Never part of an array's visible contents; `.arrayLength` excludes it.
    entry .parse   parses a tree from `Data` or from an iterator of `Data` blocks
        Four overloads, each taking the `NumberMode` in scope and raising `Parse.Error`: over a
        single `Data` or a consumed `Iterator[Data]`, each with or without `holes`, which
        admits the `j"…"` interpolator's hole markers. The underlying engine of `read[Json]`;
        public reading goes through the `Aggregable` instances.
    entry .parserAggregable   reads a bare `Json.Ast` from a stream of `Data`, raising `Parse.Error`
    entry .showable   renders a node as JSON text in the `Json.Formatting` in scope
        The single serializer: `Json.showable` and `Json.emit` both go through it. Numbers are
        written from their BCD nibbles directly, so every digit the parser saw is preserved.
    entry .inspectable   a debug rendering of a node: its compact text, marked `ᵃˢᵗ`
        Distinguishes a bare node from the `Json` document wrapping it.
    entry .Out   the writer a `Json.Printable` or the serializer writes into: text, or UTF-8 bytes
      detail
          An exclusive, stateful capability with one method per token kind. Its two
          implementations are internal: the text writer behind `show` and the pull `emit`, and
          the byte-level writer behind `emit[Data]` and `lend`. A `Json.Printable#write` body
          receives one and calls these.
      entry #ascii   writes a literal known to be ASCII: punctuation, keywords, digits
      entry #raw   writes text verbatim, escaped for nothing: the indentation unit
      entry #string   writes a JSON string literal, quoted and escaped per RFC 8259
      entry #long   writes an integer
      entry #bcdInt   writes a packed-`Int` BCD number as canonical JSON-number text
      entry #bcdLong   writes a packed-`Long` BCD number as canonical JSON-number text
      entry #bcd   writes a `Bcd` as canonical JSON-number text
    entry .Position   a value's place in the source: line and column, with its offset and length
      detail
          The `Format.Position` of JSON: `#line` and `#column` are 1-based; `#span` is the
          0-based range. A `Parse.Error` carries one for the fault, and `json.locate(pointer)`
          returns one for a value of a tracked document, spanning the whole value.
      entry #line   the 1-based line
      entry #column   the 1-based column
      entry #offset   the character offset from the start of the input, if known
      entry #length   the length of the value's text, if known
      entry #describe   the position as text: `line 2, column 3`
      entry #span   the 0-based span of the value
    entry .Issue   what can be wrong with JSON text: the reasons a `Parse.Error` carries
        Each case has a message, through `#describe`: `EmptyInput` (_the input was empty_),
        `UnexpectedChar`, `ExpectedTrue`, `ExpectedFalse`, `ExpectedNull`, `ExpectedSomeValue`,
        `ExpectedColon`, `InvalidWhitespace`, `ExpectedString`, `ExpectedHexDigit`,
        `PrematureEnd` (_the content ended prematurely_), `NumberHasLeadingZero`,
        `SpuriousContent` (something after the full value), `LeadingDecimalPoint`,
        `NotEscaped`, `IncorrectEscape`, `MultipleDecimalPoints` and `ExpectedDigit`. Four
        arise only on the direct-parsing path, where a typed reader knows what it expects:
        `ExpectedNumber`, `ExpectedBoolean`, `ExpectedObject` and `ExpectedArray`.
    entry .AsciiByte   the byte constants the tokenizer matches on
      hidden
      detail
          Not for direct use: named ASCII code points (`Comma`, `Quote`, `OpenBrace`, …) for
          the parser's byte-level matching.
    entry .isNumber   whether the node is a number, in any of its four representations
    entry .isAbsent   whether the node is the absent value, as a missing key yields
    entry .isLong   whether the node is a number held as a `Long`
    entry .isDouble   whether the node is a number held as a `Double`
    entry .isSmallBcd   whether the node is a number held as a packed `Int` of up to seven nibbles
    entry .isBcd   whether the node is a high-precision number held as a `Bcd`
    entry .isString   whether the node is a string
    entry .isBoolean   whether the node is a boolean
    entry .isNull   whether the node is an explicit JSON `null`
    entry .isObject   whether the node is an object
    entry .isArray   whether the node is an array, in any of its representations
    entry .isNumberArray   whether the node is an array stored unboxed as packed numbers
    entry .isBcdIntArray   whether the node is an array in the packed-`Int` form
    entry .isBcdLongArray   whether the node is an array in the packed-`Long` form
    entry .primitive   the node's `Json.Primitive`: `Number`, `Boolean`, `String`, `Object`, `Array` or `Null`
        An absent node reports `Null`.
    entry .arrayLength   the number of elements in an array node, excluding any padding
    entry .arrayElement   the element at an index, materialized as a node
        A packed number is decoded to a `Long` if it is an exact integer which fits, and to a
        `Double` otherwise.
    entry .objectSize   the number of key/value pairs in an object node
    entry .objectKey   the key at a pair index
    entry .objectValue   the value at a pair index
    entry .objectIndexOf   the pair index of a key, by linear scan, or `-1`
    entry .array   the elements of an array node, raising `Json.Error` for any other kind
    entry .double   the node as a `Double`, raising `Json.Error` for a non-number
    entry .bcd   the node as a `Bcd`, raising `Json.Error` for a non-number
    entry .long   the node as a `Long`, raising `Json.Error` for a non-number; a decimal is truncated
    entry .number   the node as `Long | Double | Bcd`, in whichever form holds it exactly
    entry .string   the node as `Text`, raising `Json.Error` for a non-string
    entry .boolean   the node as a `Boolean`, raising `Json.Error` for a non-boolean
  entry .Error   raised when a value cannot be read as what was asked for
    detail
        Error 337: _could not access the value because `reason`_, where the `#reason` is a
        `Json.Error.Reason`: `NotType(found, expected)` (_the JSON value had type `found`
        instead of `expected`_, each a `Json.Primitive`), `Absent` (_the JSON value was not
        present_: a required field the object lacks, or a value past the end of an array under
        `as`), and `OutOfRange` (_the array index is out of range_, from `json(index)`). It is
        raised by every `Json.Decodable`, `Json.Parsable` and `Json.Ast` accessor through the
        `Tactic[Json.Error]` in scope, and under an accruing strategy each is tagged with the
        `Json.Focus` of the field that failed.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)

        capture[Json.Error](t""""abc"""".read[Json].as[Int]).reason
        // Json.Error.Reason.NotType(Json.Primitive.String, Json.Primitive.Number)

        capture[Json.Error](t"{}".read[Json].as[Person]).reason   // Json.Error.Reason.Absent
        ```

        Malformed JSON *text* is a different error: `Parse.Error`, from zephyrine, with a
        `Json.Ast.Position` and a `Json.Ast.Issue`. A `Json.Provider` record field raises
        `Json.Provider.Error` instead.
    refer jacinta.Json.Primitive
    refer jacinta.Json.Focus
    entry .Reason   why a value could not be read: `OutOfRange`, `NotType(found, expected)` or `Absent`
      detail
          An enumeration with a number per case (1, 2 and 3), rendered by `.communicable`.
      entry .communicable   renders each reason as a message
      entry #number   the reason's number within error 337
    entry #reason   the `Json.Error.Reason`
  entry .Primitive   the six kinds of JSON value: `Array`, `Object`, `Number`, `Null`, `Boolean` and `String`
    detail
        What `Json.Ast.primitive` reports, and what `Json.Error.Reason.NotType` names on each
        side. `.communicable` renders one as a message.
    entry .communicable   renders a primitive's name as a message
  entry .DiscriminantField   the tagged-union shape where the tag is a field beside the variant's own
    detail
        `{"radius": 1.0, "type": "Circle"}`: a `Discriminable in Json` whose tag is a field of
        the variant's own object, under the label given. `Json.discriminatedUnion(label)`
        builds one, and `discriminables.jsonByTypeDiscriminable` and
        `discriminables.jsonByKindDiscriminable` are the `type` and `kind` instances. Direct
        parsing finds the tag with a bounded scan-ahead, so it need not come first; this is
        the one shape `Json.Parsable.staged` supports for sums.
    entry #rewrite   attaches the tag field to a variant's encoding
    entry #discriminate   reads the tag field, or `Unset` when there is none
    entry #variant   the variant's own fields, with the tag removed
  entry .DiscriminantWrapper   the tagged-union shape where the variant sits inside a single-key object named for it
    detail
        `{"Circle": {"radius": 1.0}}`: the streaming-friendliest shape, since the tag is always
        the first token. `discriminables.jsonWrapperDiscriminable` is the instance for every
        type; an object with more than one key is not a valid variant and raises
        `Json.Error(Absent)` rather than guessing which key was meant.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import formatting.compactJsonFormatting

        enum Shape:
          case Circle(radius: Double)
          case Square(side: Double)

        given Shape is Discriminable in Json = Json.DiscriminantWrapper()
        (Shape.Circle(1.0): Shape).in[Json].show   // t"""{"Circle":{"radius":1.0}}"""
        ```
    entry #rewrite   wraps a variant's encoding in an object keyed by its tag
    entry #discriminate   the single key, or `Unset` for anything but a one-key object
    entry #variant   the value under the single key
  entry .DiscriminantEnvelope   the tagged-union shape with a tag field and a payload field side by side
    detail
        `{"type": "Circle", "value": {"radius": 1.0}}`, with the two field names given.
        `discriminables.jsonEnvelopeDiscriminable` is the `type`/`value` instance. Direct
        parsing does not require the tag to come first.
    entry #rewrite   encloses a variant's encoding in an envelope with its tag
    entry #discriminate   the tag field's string value, or `Unset`
    entry #variant   the payload field
  entry #positionIndex   the `Json.PositionIndex` of a tracked document, or `Unset`
      Present only for a document parsed with `parsing.trackPositions` in scope; `locate` and
      `locateKey` read it.
  entry #apply   `json(t"key")` selects a field, and `json(index)` an array element
      `json(t"key")` yields the field's value, or an absent `Json` (which decodes to `Unset`
      and raises `Absent` for anything else) when the object lacks it. `json(index)` yields
      the element at a zero-based index, raising `Json.Error(OutOfRange)` past the end and
      `NotType(…, Array)` for a non-array. Both take a `Tactic[Json.Error]`. For a document
      `verify`'d against a type, `json(index)` is a macro which types the element by the
      schema.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val data = t"""{"a": {"b": {"c": 42}}}""".read[Json]
      data(t"a")(t"b")(t"c").as[Int]   // 42
      t"[10, 20, 30]".read[Json](1).as[Int]   // 20
      ```
  entry #selectDynamic   `json.field`: a field read as a member, with dynamic access enabled
      A macro. For a plain `Json` it needs a `Json is Dynamical` in scope —
      `dynamicAccess.dynamicJson` imported, or a `dynamically[Json]` block — and reads the
      field at runtime, yielding an absent `Json` for a missing key; for a document
      `verify`'d against a type it checks that the type has the field, needs no enabler, and
      types the result by the schema. A field of an unverified document without the enabler
      is a compile error which says so.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import dynamicAccess.dynamicJson

      val person = t"""{"name": "Hal"}""".read[Json]
      person.name.as[Text]              // t"Hal"
      person.missing.as[Optional[Int]]  // Unset
      ```
  entry #applyDynamic   `json.field(index)`: an element of an array field, with dynamic access enabled
      The macro behind `json.items(2)`: selects the field and then the element, with the same
      enabler rule and verified-document typing as `#selectDynamic`.
  entry #update   `json(index) = value`: a new document with one array element replaced
      Needs dynamic access enabled, a `Json.Error` tactic (raised for a non-array), and a value
      `Encodable in Json`.
  entry #updateDynamic   `json.field = value` sets or replaces a field, and `json.field = Unset` removes one
      Two overloads, each needing dynamic access enabled (`dynamicAccess.dynamicJson` or a
      `dynamically[Json]` block) and returning a new document: the original is unchanged. The
      value may be anything `Encodable in Json`. Neither raises: applying either to a
      non-object `Json` is a programming error, which throws rather than raising `Json.Error`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import formatting.compactJsonFormatting
      import dynamicAccess.dynamicJson

      val base = t"""{"x": 1}""".read[Json]
      (base.y = 2).show        // t"""{"x":1,"y":2}"""
      (base.x = Unset).show    // t"""{}"""
      ```
  entry #as   decodes the value to a Scala type: `json.as[Person]`
      Resolves a `Decodable in Json` for the type — any `Json.Decodable`, so the primitives,
      collections, `Optional`, and every case class and sum by derivation — and needs a
      `Tactic[Json.Error]` and a `Foci[Json.Focus]`. After decoding, the focus of any error
      registered under an accruing strategy is enriched with the value's source position, for
      a tracked document. `read[T in Json]` on the source is the one-step form.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      t"42".read[Json].as[Int]                              // 42
      t"[1, 2, 3]".read[Json].as[List[Int]]                 // List(1, 2, 3)
      t"""{"a": 1, "b": 2}""".read[Json].as[Map[Text, Int]] // Map(t"a" -> 1, t"b" -> 2)
      t"null".read[Json].as[Unit]                           // ()
      ```

entry .Json1   the mixin holding the direct-path givens of `Json`'s companion
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: a layer of `object Json`'s given-priority chain, above `Json2`.
      Holds `aggregableParsed`, `readableParsed`, `encodableOverPrinted`,
      `aggregableStrictFailure` and `encodableOverStrictFailure`, documented below;
      `object Json` inherits them.
  entry #aggregableParsed   `read[T in Json]` directly from a stream, whenever `T` has a `Json.Parsable`
      The direct read path, above `.aggregableDirect`: the input, a single block or a stream,
      is parsed in place through a `Json.Reader`, and no tree is built for what the instance
      reads directly. Under `parsing.trackPositions` parse errors carry real source
      coordinates. Needs a `Tactic[Parse.Error]` and a `PositionTracking`.
  entry #readableParsed   `data.read[T in Json]` directly from a whole `Data`, without wrapping it in a stream
  entry #encodableOverPrinted   `value.in[Text over Json]` written directly by a `Json.Printable`, when compact
      Above `.encodableOver`: a type with a `Json.Printable` is written straight to text,
      with no tree, whenever the `Json.Formatting` in scope is compact; indented formatting
      still renders through the tree, which generated printers do not write.
  entry #aggregableStrictFailure   the compile error of `codecs.strict` for a type with no direct parser
  entry #encodableOverStrictFailure   the compile error of `codecs.strict` for a type with no direct printer

entry .Json2   the mixin holding the derived-path and element-wise givens of `Json`'s companion
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: a layer of `object Json`'s given-priority chain, above `Json3`.
      Holds `decodable`, `encodable`, `optional`, `optionalEncodable`, `bytes`, the
      `field…` instances, `optionalParsable`, `encodableOver`, `encodableOverData`,
      `aggregableDirect` and the three derivation objects, documented below;
      `object Json` inherits them.
  entry #optionalEncodable   encodes an `Optional[T]`: `Unset` encodes as absent, so an object omits the field
  entry #optional   decodes an `Optional[T]` field: absent or `null` reads as `Unset`, by the policies in scope
      An absent key reads as `Unset`, an explicit `null` reads as `Unset`, and a value the
      inner decoder rejects raises. Each is a policy, read from a given at resolution:
      `Decodable.Absence in Json`, `Decodable.Nullity in Json` and `Decodable.Fault in Json`,
      whose defaults are those three, and whose alternatives are the `optionalityOptions`
      package's `strictJsonAbsence` (absent raises `Absent`), `strictJsonNulls` (a `null` is
      handed to the inner decoder) and `lenientJsonFaults` (a rejected value reads as `Unset`,
      under the ambient tactic's `tolerate`, so nothing accrues).
  entry #bytes   decodes a JSON number to `Bytes`, a count of bytes
  entry #fieldOptional   reads an `Optional[T]` field directly, by the `optionalityOptions` policies in scope
  entry #fieldOption   reads an `Option[T]` field directly
  entry #fieldArray   reads any `Iterable` collection field directly, with a `Factory` in scope
  entry #fieldList   reads a `List[T]` field directly
  entry #fieldSet   reads a `Set[T]` field directly
  entry #fieldSeries   reads a `Sequence[T]` field directly
  entry #fieldMap   reads a `Map[K, V]` field directly, decoding each key from text
  entry #optionalParsable   an `Optional[T]` parses directly when `T` has opted in
  entry #encodableOver   `value.in[Text over Json]`: a value's JSON text, through the tree
      The write-side mirror of `read[T in Json]`: encodes to a `Json` and renders it in the
      `Json.Formatting` in scope, as `show` would. Outranked by `.encodableOverPrinted`
      wherever a `Json.Printable` exists and the formatting is compact.
  entry #encodableOverData   `value.in[Data over Json]`: the same text as bytes, in the `Codepage` in scope
  entry #aggregableDirect   `read[T in Json]` through the tree: parse the whole input, then decode
      The read path for a type with a `Json.Decodable` but no `Json.Parsable`: the input is
      parsed into a `Json` and decoded with `as[T]`. Needs a `Tactic[Parse.Error]`, a
      `Tactic[Json.Error]` and a `PositionTracking`. `.aggregableParsed` wins whenever the
      type has a `Json.Parsable`.
  entry #decodable   the universal `Json.Decodable`: `Json` itself, a text codec, or derivation from the type's shape
      `[value] => value is Json.Decodable`: a value is `Json` itself; or has a `Decodable in
      Text`, in which case a JSON string is decoded through it; or is a case class, enumeration
      or sealed hierarchy, derived by `.DecodableDerivation`. A hand-written `Json.Decodable`
      in the type's companion outranks it.
  entry #encodable   the universal `Json.Encodable`: a text codec, or derivation from the type's shape
      `[value] => value is Json.Encodable`. A type with an `Encodable in Text` encodes as a JSON
      string; a case class encodes as an object of its fields (renamed by `@name` or
      `@name[Json]`, `Optional`/`Option` fields omitted when absent); an enumeration or sealed
      hierarchy encodes as its variant, tagged by the `Discriminable in Json` in scope. A
      hand-written instance in the type's companion outranks it.
  entry #DecodableDerivation   derives a `Json.Decodable` for a case class or a sum, through wisteria
    detail
        Not for direct use: `.decodable` reaches it. A product decodes from an object, reading
        each field at its own `Json.Focus` so that every missing or mistyped field accrues, with
        `@name` renames applied and declared defaults filling absent keys; a sum reads the
        discriminator through the `Discriminable in Json` in scope (the `discriminables`
        package, or a `Json.DiscriminantField`/`DiscriminantWrapper`/`DiscriminantEnvelope` in
        the type's companion) and decodes the variant it names.
    entry .conjunction   the product derivation: an object with one field per constructor parameter
    entry .disjunction   the sum derivation: a tagged variant, by the `Discriminable in Json` in scope
  entry #ParsableDerivation   derives a direct parser (`Json.Field`) for a case class or a sum
    detail
        Not for direct use: `Json.Parsable.derived` and the `.field` fallback reach it. A
        product parses through `Json.Parsable.product`, dispatching on keys through a
        `Json.KeyTable`; a sum chooses its token-level strategy by the shape of the
        `Discriminable in Json`: a `DiscriminantWrapper`'s tag is its first token, an
        envelope's and a field's are found with a bounded scan-ahead, and any other
        `Discriminable` materializes one value and decodes it.
    entry .conjunction   the product derivation
    entry .disjunction   the sum derivation
  entry #EncodableDerivation   derives a `Json.Encodable` for a case class or a sum, through wisteria
    detail
        Not for direct use: `.encodable` reaches it. A product encodes as an object, omitting a
        field whose encoding is absent (an `Unset` or `None`); a sum encodes its variant and
        tags it with the `Discriminable in Json` in scope, renaming the variant by `@name`.
    entry .conjunction   the product derivation
    entry .disjunction   the sum derivation

entry .Json3   the mixin holding the lowest-priority fallbacks of `Json`'s companion
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: holds `field`, the universal `Json.Field` fallback, and
      `decodableAtFocus`, the adapter `Json#as` resolves, documented below;
      `object Json` inherits them.
  entry #field   the universal field parser: a direct parser, a text codec, derivation, or the tree bridge
      Mirrors `.decodable`'s order so that a field's wire format is identical on both paths:
      an explicit or derived `Json.Parsable`; a `Decodable in Text`, reading a JSON string; a
      case class or sum, derived by `.ParsableDerivation`; and last, any remaining
      `Json.Decodable` through `Json.Parsable.fromDecodable`. A type with both a `Reflection`
      and a custom `Json.Decodable` derives here, diverging from its decoder; the remedy is a
      `Json.Parsable.fromDecodable` instance in its companion.
  entry #decodableAtFocus   lifts any `Decodable in Json` to one carrying a `Json.Focus`, for `as[T]`
      The lowest-priority given, and the only one which produces the `at Json.Focus` form
      `Json#as` summons, so a generic caller bounded on `Decodable in Json` still resolves.

entry .Json4   the mixin holding `Json.Provider`
  detail
      Not for direct use: the layer of `object Json`'s companion chain which holds the type
      provider over a JSON Schema document, documented below; `object Json` inherits
      it, so it is used as `Json.Provider`.
  entry #Provider   a type provider over a JSON Schema document: typed records from matching JSON
    detail
        `object Catalogue extends Json.Provider(schema)` binds a JSON Schema, read as the code
        compiles, to a structurally-typed record with one field per property of the schema's
        root object, each read at the type its schema declares. `Catalogue.record(json)` turns
        a `Json` into such a record, and `Catalogue.tuple(json)` into a named tuple whose
        elements are all read at once. The schema is `into[Json.Provider.Schema]`: a `Json`,
        or anything readable as JSON, such as a classpath resource (`cp"/schemas/x.json"`, with
        a `Classloader` in scope) or JSON text.

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        object Catalogue extends Json.Provider(t"""{
          "type": "object",
          "required": ["name", "children"],
          "properties": {
            "name": { "type": "string" },
            "email": { "type": "string", "format": "email" },
            "children": {
              "type": "array",
              "items": {
                "type": "object",
                "required": ["weight"],
                "properties": { "weight": { "type": "number", "minimum": 0 } }
              }
            }
          }
        }""".read[Json])

        val input = t"""{"name": "Bicycle", "children": [{"weight": 9.5}]}""".read[Json]
        val record = Catalogue.record(input)
        record.name                          // t"Bicycle"
        record.children.head.let(_.weight)   // 9.5
        record.nope                          // does not compile: the schema has no such property
        ```

        A property in the schema's `required` list reads at its type and raises
        `Json.Provider.Error(MissingValue(name))` when the document omits it; any other, or
        one whose type admits `null`, reads as an `Optional`. An `array` reads as a `List` of
        its `items` type, an `object` with `properties` as a nested record, an object whose
        values one schema describes (`additionalProperties`, or `patternProperties` of one
        type) as a `Map[Text, T]`, and a property whose name is not an identifier through a
        backticked name. A local `$ref` is followed, an `allOf` of objects merges their
        properties, and `anyOf`/`oneOf` alternatives (or a `type` naming several) read as a
        Scala union chosen by the kind of the value found. A string's `enum`/`const`,
        `pattern`, `minLength`/`maxLength` and a number's bounds are checked as the field is
        read, through the readers `.boundedInteger`, `.boundedNumber`, `.boundedString`,
        `.pattern` and `.enumeration`, and a value of the wrong JSON type raises
        `JsonType(expected, found)`; every error goes through the `Tactic` of the code reading
        the field, so `validate` with a `venture` per field collects every problem.

        What stays raw `Json`: alternatives a value's kind cannot tell apart (two object
        shapes in a `oneOf`), `not` and `if`, an object with neither `properties` nor a value
        schema, an array with no `items` or a tuple-form `items`, a reference to another
        document, and a recursive reference at its point of recursion. A root which is not an
        object with properties is a compile error. A property named as a method of every JVM
        object (`notify`, `toString`, …) is read through `record.selectDynamic("notify")`.

        A format naming a domain type — `date-time`, `email`, `hostname`, `ipv4`, `uri`,
        `uuid`, … — reads as whichever type represents that domain at the call site, chosen by
        importing its interface: `emailAddressInterfaces.soundnessEmailAddress` makes an
        `email` field an `EmailAddress`, `uuidInterfaces.soundnessUuid` a `uuid` field a `Uuid`,
        and so on. The readers are givens of `.Reader` in this companion.
    refer jacinta.Json.Provider.Error
    refer jacinta.JsonSchema
    entry .Reader   the type of a provider's fallible readers: an `Intensional.Reading` raising `Json.Provider.Error`
        `Json.Provider.Reader[label, result]` is a reading of a `Json` labelled by the
        schema's type or format name, through the `Tactic[Json.Provider.Error]` of the code
        reading the field. Every reader below is one.
    entry .boolean   reads a `boolean` property as `Boolean`
    entry .string   reads a `string` property as `Text`
    entry .integer   reads an `integer` property as `Int`
    entry .long   reads an `integer` of format `int64` as `Long`
    entry .number   reads a `number` property as `Double`
    entry .json   reads a property the schema does not constrain to one type, as the raw `Json`
    entry .dateTime   reads a `date-time` string through the `Instantiable across Instants` in scope
    entry .date   reads a `date` string through the `Instantiable across Dates` in scope
    entry .time   reads a `time` string through the `Instantiable across Times` in scope
    entry .duration   reads a `duration` string through the `Instantiable across Durations` in scope
    entry .uriReference   reads a `uri-reference` string as `Text`
    entry .email   reads an `email` string through the `Instantiable across EmailAddresses` in scope
    entry .idnEmail   reads an `idn-email` string through the `Instantiable across EmailAddresses` in scope
    entry .hostname   reads a `hostname` string through the `Instantiable across Hostnames` in scope
    entry .ipv4   reads an `ipv4` string through the `Instantiable across IpAddresses` in scope
    entry .ipv6   reads an `ipv6` string through the `Instantiable across IpAddresses` in scope
    entry .uri   reads a `uri` string through the `Instantiable across Urls` in scope
    entry .iri   reads an `iri` string through the `Instantiable across Urls` in scope
    entry .iriReference   reads an `iri-reference` string as `Text`
    entry .uuid   reads a `uuid` string through the `Instantiable across Uuids` in scope
    entry .uriTemplate   reads a `uri-template` string as `Text`
    entry .jsonPointer   reads a `json-pointer` string as a `JsonPointer`, raising `Unparsable` if it does not parse
    entry .regex   reads a `regex` string as a `Regex`, raising `Unparsable` if it does not compile
    entry .BoundedInteger   the reader of an `integer` with `minimum`/`maximum` bounds, raising `IntOutOfRange`
    entry .boundedInteger   the `BoundedInteger` given
    entry .BoundedNumber   the reader of a `number` with bounds, exclusive or inclusive, raising `NumberOutOfRange`
    entry .boundedNumber   the `BoundedNumber` given
    entry .BoundedString   the reader of a `string` with `minLength`/`maxLength`, raising `LengthOutOfRange`
    entry .boundedString   the `BoundedString` given
    entry .Pattern   the reader of a `string` with a `pattern`, raising `PatternMismatch`
    entry .pattern   the `Pattern` given
    entry .Enumeration   the reader of a `string` with `enum` or `const`, raising `NotPermitted`
    entry .enumeration   the `Enumeration` given
    entry .Primitives   the format's reading primitives without a schema: what a provider adds its fields to
      detail
          A polyvinyl `Specification` over `Json` whose failure type is `Json.Provider.Error`.
          `Json.Provider` extends it, and a macro with fields of its own (an OpenAPI
          `record()`) builds records over it. Its methods are the hooks the record macro calls
          to access, test and enumerate JSON values.
      entry #access   the value under a name, absent rather than failing for a key the object lacks
      entry #absent   whether a value is absent or `null`; a `null` type marks a field optional
      entry #required   the value under a name, raising `MissingValue(name)` when absent
      entry #kind   the kind of a value, by which a union chooses its alternative
      entry #elements   the elements of an array value
      entry #pairs   the key/value pairs of an object value
      entry #entries   the pairs of the object under a name
      entry #repeated   the elements of the array under a name, empty where absent
      entry #unoffered   raises `UnexpectedKind` for a union value of a kind no alternative offers
    entry .fieldsOf   the fields of a schema's root object, as a provider's specification
        Raises `Json.Error` through its tactic for a schema which cannot be read; a caller at
        macro time wraps it in `safely(…).or(halt(…))`.
    entry .memberOf   the member reading the values a node within a larger document describes
        Its `$ref`s resolve against the document, so a schema embedded in one — a response
        schema in an OpenAPI document referring to `#/components/schemas/…` — reads as it
        would alone. An object reads as a record, an array of objects as a repeated one.
        `limit` bounds how many references deep the walk follows before reading a reference
        as raw `Json`.
    entry .Schema   the schema a provider is built from: a `Json`, or anything readable as one
      detail
          The `into[Json.Provider.Schema]` parameter of `Json.Provider` converts its argument
          through the givens here: `.json` for a `Json` already parsed, and `.readable` for
          any source `Readable to Json`, such as a classpath resource or JSON text, read as the
          code compiles.
          The companion inherits `readable` from `.Schema2`.
      entry .json   a `Json` as a schema
      entry #json   the schema document
    entry .Schema2   the mixin holding the lower-priority `readable` conversion
      hidden
      detail
          Not for direct use; see `Json.Provider.Schema`.
      entry #readable   any source readable as `Json` as a schema
    entry .Error   raised when a document does not conform to a provider's schema
      detail
          Error 624: _the JSON was not valid according to the schema because `reason`_, raised
          through the `Tactic` of the code reading a record field, so `attempt`, `recover`,
          `validate` and `track` all see it. The `#reason` is a `Json.Provider.Error.Reason`:
          `JsonType(expected, found)`, `MissingValue(name)` (a required property absent),
          `IntOutOfRange(value, minimum, maximum)`, `PatternMismatch(value, pattern)`,
          `NumberOutOfRange(value, minimum, maximum)`, `LengthOutOfRange(value, minimum,
          maximum)`, `NotPermitted(value, permitted)` (not in the `enum`), `UnexpectedKind(found,
          offered)` (a union value of a kind no alternative offers) and `Unparsable(value,
          format)` (a `json-pointer` or `regex` which does not parse).
      entry .Reason   why a document did not conform, numbered 1 to 9 within error 624
        detail
            An enumeration of the nine reasons, rendered as messages by its companion's
            anonymous `Communicable`.
        entry #number   the reason's number
      entry #reason   the `Json.Provider.Error.Reason`
    entry #schema   the schema document the provider was built from
    entry #fields   the schema's root properties as polyvinyl members, raising `Specification.Error`
        A schema which cannot be read is raised, and the `record`/`tuple` macros halt with its
        message at the use site.

entry .JsonPointer   an RFC 6901 JSON Pointer: a path into a document, written `jp"#/a/0/b"`
  detail
      A `JsonPointer` names a location within a document as a path of keys and array indices
      on the `JsonPointer` plane: the object is the plane's root, and a pointer is built
      segment by segment with `#apply`, or written with the `jp"…"` interpolator, which checks
      it as the code compiles. It encodes as `#/a/b` (the empty pointer as `#`), with `~` and
      `/` within a segment escaped as `~0` and `~1`, and decodes from the same text, raising
      `JsonPointer.Error` with the offset of the fault.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      jp"#/verona/capulet".encode   // t"#/verona/capulet"
      JsonPointer()(t"a")(t"b").encode   // t"#/a/b"
      (JsonPointer() / t"a" / Prim).encode   // t"#/a/0"
      t"#/x/y".as[JsonPointer]
      ```

      Pointers are how `Json.Focus` names the field an error was raised at, how `locate` and
      `locateKey` address a tracked document's positions, and how a `Json.Provider` follows a
      `$ref`.
  refer jacinta.jp
  refer jacinta.Json.Focus
  entry .Plane   the plane type of pointer paths: `JsonPointer` itself
  entry .navigable   an `Ordinal` as a pointer segment, rendered as its zero-based index
  entry .admissible   any `Ordinal` is a permitted segment
  entry .admissible2   any `Text` is a permitted segment
  entry .filesystem   the pointer plane as a `Filesystem`: `/`-separated, rooted at `#`, with `~0`/`~1` escapes
  entry .interpolable   the macro behind `jp"…"`: a pointer literal parsed as the code compiles
      Reuses `.decodable` at expansion, so a malformed literal is a compile error at the
      offending character: `jp"#/verona~2capulet"` does not compile.
  entry .decodable   reads a pointer from text, raising `JsonPointer.Error` with the offset of the fault
      The text must begin with `#` (`ExpectedHash`), a non-empty pointer must continue with
      `/` (`ExpectedSlash`), and every `~` must be followed by `0` or `1` (`BadEscape`). A
      URL-bearing reference is rejected: same-document references are all the library follows.
  entry .unsafeParse   decodes a pointer whose text is already known to be valid, for the interpolator
      Takes the `Unsafe` token: the `jp"…"` macro has parsed the literal at expansion, so the
      runtime re-parse cannot fail.
  entry .divisible   `pointer / t"key"`: a pointer extended by a key segment
  entry .divisible2   `pointer / ordinal`: a pointer extended by an array index
  entry .Error   raised when text is not a valid JSON reference
    detail
        Error 415: _the JSON reference was not valid because `reason`_, with the `#offset` of
        the character at fault, which the `jp"…"` interpolator uses to position its compile
        error. The reasons are `ExpectedHash` (_a JSON reference must begin with '#'_),
        `ExpectedSlash` (_a JSON reference fragment must begin with '/'_) and `BadEscape` (_a
        '~' in a JSON reference must be followed by '0' or '1'_).
    entry .Reason   why the reference was invalid: `ExpectedHash`, `ExpectedSlash` or `BadEscape`
      detail
          Numbered 2, 3 and 4 within error 415, rendered by `.communicable`.
      entry #number   the reason's number
    entry .communicable   renders each reason as a message
    entry #reason   the `JsonPointer.Error.Reason`
    entry #offset   the character index within the reference text where the error was detected
  entry #path   the pointer as a `Path on JsonPointer`
  entry #apply   the pointer extended by a key (`Text`) or an index (`Ordinal`)

entry .NumberMode   what becomes of a JSON number too long for the parser's fast path
  detail
      A number of up to about fifteen digits is decoded identically whatever the mode, as a
      `Long` or a `Double`. The mode decides the rest, and is a given chosen by importing one
      of the `numberModes` package by name; `.default` is `Full`.

      `Full` keeps every digit, accumulating them as a binary-coded decimal (`Bcd`) at the cost
      of one allocation for that number: the default, because losing digits silently is the
      wrong thing to do by default. `Double` drops the overflow and computes an IEEE-754
      `Double` from the leading digits — what Jawn, Circe, Jsoniter and Jackson do — for a
      like-for-like throughput comparison, or data known to be within a double's range. `Bcd`
      also drops the overflow but emits the raw packed accumulator as a `Long` with no
      allocation at all: the `Long` carries BCD nibbles rather than the number's value, so
      those numbers must be treated as opaque.

      ```scala fuel
      alternative `Full`
        code
            import soundness.*
            import strategies.throwUnsafely
            import numberModes.fullNumberMode

            Json.unseal(t"12345678901234567890".read[Json]).isBcd   // true
      alternative `Double`
        code
            import soundness.*
            import strategies.throwUnsafely
            import numberModes.doubleNumberMode

            Json.unseal(t"12345678901234567890".read[Json]).isDouble   // true
      ##
      ```
  refer jacinta.numberModes
  entry .default   the mode in scope when none is imported: `Full`

entry .Bcd   hypotenuse's binary-coded decimal, exported here: the exact form of a long JSON number
    `export hypotenuse.Bcd`. A number with more digits than a `Long` or `Double` can hold
    parses to a `Bcd` under the default `NumberMode`, and `Json.Ast.bcd` reads any number as
    one. Its own page is in the `hypotenuse` package.

entry .j   the `j"…"` interpolator: a JSON literal checked as it compiles, with holes, spreads and merges
    `j"""{"a": $x}"""` is a `Json` whose syntax was checked while the code compiled — a
    trailing comma, say, is reported at the character — and whose holes are filled at runtime.
    A hole substitutes any value `Encodable in Json`; inside an array, `$xs*` splices every
    element of a collection; among an object's fields, a `$map` hole merges a `Map[Text, Json]`.
    In a `match`, the same interpolator is a pattern which binds its holes.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    val x = 42
    val xs = List(2, 3, 4)
    val rest: Map[Text, Json] = Map(t"b" -> 2.in[Json], t"c" -> 3.in[Json])

    j"""{"a": $x}"""         // {"a": 42}
    j"""[1, $xs*]"""         // [1, 2, 3, 4]
    j"""{"a": 1, $rest}"""   // {"a": 1, "b": 2, "c": 3}

    j"""[1, 2, 3, 4]""" match
      case j"""[$head, $tail*]""" => (head, tail)
      case _                      => (j"0", j"[]")

    j""" {"a": 1, } """   // does not compile: a trailing comma
    ```

entry .jp   the `jp"…"` interpolator: a JSON Pointer literal checked as it compiles
    `jp"#/verona/capulet"` is a `JsonPointer`, parsed while the code compiles so that a malformed
    pointer — one not beginning with `#`, or with a bad `~` escape — is a compile error at the
    offending character. The interpolator takes no substitutions.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    jp"#/verona/capulet".encode   // t"#/verona/capulet"
    jp"#".encode           // t"#"
    jp"#/verona~2capulet"         // does not compile: ~2 is not a valid escape
    ```

entry .formatting   the two standard `Json.Formatting`s, each a given to import by name
  detail
      `show`, `emit`, `in[Text over Json]` and the HTTP givens all need a `Json.Formatting`,
      and `import soundness.*` brings none in; one is imported by name. A custom one is
      `Json.Formatting(indent, trailingNewline)`.

      ```scala fuel
      alternative Compact
        code
            import soundness.*
            import formatting.compactJsonFormatting

            Json.make(a = 1.in[Json], b = List(2, 3).in[Json]).show   // t"""{"a":1,"b":[2,3]}"""
      alternative Indented
        code
            import soundness.*
            import formatting.indentedJsonFormatting

            Json.make(a = 1.in[Json], b = List(2, 3).in[Json]).show   // across six lines
      ##
      ```
  entry .compactJsonFormatting   no whitespace, no trailing newline: `{"a":1}`
  entry .indentedJsonFormatting   two spaces per nesting level, newlines between members, no trailing newline

entry .numberModes   the three `NumberMode`s, each a given to import by name
  detail
      How a number too long for a `Long` or `Double` is parsed: see `NumberMode`. Without an
      import the default is `fullNumberMode`.
  entry .fullNumberMode   every digit kept as a `Bcd`; the default
  entry .doubleNumberMode   the leading digits as a `Double`, as Jackson and Circe do
  entry .bcdNumberMode   the raw packed accumulator as an opaque `Long`, with no allocation

entry .optionalityOptions   how an `Optional` or `Option` field reads an absent key, a `null`, or a rejected value
  detail
      An `Optional` (or `Option`) field answers three questions, each a separate given with a
      default: an absent key reads as `Unset` (`lenientJsonAbsence`, the default; `strictJsonAbsence`
      raises `Json.Error(Absent)`); an explicit `null` reads as `Unset` (`lenientJsonNulls`, the
      default; `strictJsonNulls` hands it to the inner decoder, which rejects it as it would for
      any other field); and a value the inner decoder rejects is an error (`strictJsonFaults`,
      the default; `lenientJsonFaults` reads it as `Unset`, under the ambient tactic's
      `tolerate`, so nothing accrues). Each can be varied alone. The direct and staged parsers
      honour the same policies, with one limit: a lenient fault is tolerated only when the
      value on the wire is of the wrong kind, which the parser can skip whole; a value of the
      right kind that fails within it still raises. A declared default of `Unset` is
      indistinguishable from no default, so it does not satisfy a strict absence.

      ```scala fuel
      alternative Defaults
        code
            import soundness.*
            import strategies.throwUnsafely

            case class Profile(name: Text, age: Optional[Int])

            t"""{"name": "Nym"}""".read[Json].as[Profile].age                   // Unset
            t"""{"name": "Nym", "age": null}""".read[Json].as[Profile].age      // Unset
            capture[Json.Error](t"""{"name": "Nym", "age": "old"}""".read[Json].as[Profile])
      alternative Strict absence
        code
            import soundness.*
            import strategies.throwUnsafely
            import optionalityOptions.strictJsonAbsence

            case class Profile(name: Text, age: Optional[Int])

            capture[Json.Error](t"""{"name": "Nym"}""".read[Json].as[Profile]).reason
            // Json.Error.Reason.Absent
      alternative Strict nulls
        code
            import soundness.*
            import strategies.throwUnsafely
            import optionalityOptions.strictJsonNulls

            case class Profile(name: Text, age: Optional[Int])

            capture[Json.Error](t"""{"name": "Nym", "age": null}""".read[Json].as[Profile]).reason
            // Json.Error.Reason.NotType(Json.Primitive.Null, Json.Primitive.Number)
      alternative Lenient faults
        code
            import soundness.*
            import strategies.throwUnsafely
            import optionalityOptions.lenientJsonFaults

            case class Profile(name: Text, age: Optional[Int])

            t"""{"name": "Nym", "age": "old"}""".read[Json].as[Profile].age   // Unset
      ##
      ```
  entry .strictJsonAbsence   an absent key raises `Json.Error(Absent)` rather than reading as `Unset`
  entry .lenientJsonAbsence   an absent key reads as `Unset`; the default
  entry .strictJsonNulls   a `null` is handed to the inner decoder, which rejects it
  entry .lenientJsonNulls   a `null` reads as `Unset`; the default
  entry .strictJsonFaults   a value the inner decoder rejects raises; the default
  entry .lenientJsonFaults   a value the inner decoder rejects reads as `Unset`, and nothing accrues

entry .discriminables   the standard wire shapes of a tagged union, each a given to import by name
  detail
      An enumeration or sealed hierarchy encodes as its variant plus a tag, and the
      `Discriminable in Json` in scope says where the tag goes. None is in scope by default;
      one is imported by name, or a `Json.DiscriminantField`, `Json.DiscriminantWrapper` or
      `Json.DiscriminantEnvelope` is given in the type's companion. Each works through the
      tree and through direct parsing; `Json.Parsable.staged` supports only the field shapes.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import formatting.compactJsonFormatting
      import discriminables.jsonByKindDiscriminable

      enum Shape:
        case Circle(radius: Double)
        case Square(side: Double)

      (Shape.Circle(1.0): Shape).in[Json].show                       // t"""{"radius":1.0,"kind":"Circle"}"""
      t"""{"radius":1.0,"kind":"Circle"}""".read[Json].as[Shape]   // Shape.Circle(1.0)
      ```

      `@name` renames a case's tag value just as it renames a field.
  entry .jsonByTypeDiscriminable   the tag in a `type` field beside the variant's own: `{"radius":1.0,"type":"Circle"}`
  entry .jsonByKindDiscriminable   the tag in a `kind` field beside the variant's own: `{"radius":1.0,"kind":"Circle"}`
  entry .jsonWrapperDiscriminable   the variant inside an object keyed by its tag: `{"Circle":{"radius":1.0}}`
      The streaming-friendliest shape, since the tag is always the first token.
  entry .jsonEnvelopeDiscriminable   a `type` field and a `value` field: `{"type":"Circle","value":{"radius":1.0}}`

entry .dynamicAccess   the given which licenses `json.field` on an unverified document
  detail
      A `Json` is dynamically typed, and the compiler knows nothing of its fields, so reading
      one as a member is a real loss of checking and is not on by default. `import
      dynamicAccess.dynamicJson` grants the permission, `Json is Dynamical`, to the rest of its
      scope; the same permission is confined to a block with `dynamically[Json]:`, or
      `dynamically:` for every format with dynamic access. A document `verify`'d against a type
      navigates its fields without either, since the schema licenses it.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val config = t"""{"server": {"port": 8080}}""".read[Json]

      dynamically[Json]:
        config.server.port.as[Int]   // 8080
      ```
  entry .dynamicJson   `Json is Dynamical`: enables `json.field`, `json.field = value` and `json.lens(…)` in its scope

entry .conversions   a `Conversion` from any `Encodable in Json` value to `Json`, to import by name
  detail
      With `conversions.encodableToJson` imported, a value with an encoder may be supplied
      wherever a `Json` is expected — `Json.make(a = 1)`, or a lens assignment
      `json.lens(_.name = t"x")` — without writing `.in[Json]`.
  entry .encodableToJson   `Conversion[T, Json]` for any `T` that is `Encodable in Json`

entry .postables   sends a `Json` as an `application/json` request body; JVM only
  detail
      `jacinta.http`. The given needs a `Codepage` and a `Json.Formatting`, and renders the
      document with `show`, encoded in the codepage, under the media type `application/json;
      charset=UTF-8`.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import postables.jsonPostable
      url"https://example.com/api".post(Json.make(name = t"Hal".in[Json]))
      ```
  entry .jsonPostable   `Json is Postable`: a request body of the document's text, as `application/json`

entry .servables   serves a `Json` as an `application/json` response body; JVM only
  detail
      `jacinta.http`. The given needs a `Codepage` and a `Json.Formatting`, and renders the
      document with `show` into a fixed-length response body.
  entry .jsonServable   `Json is Servable`: a response of the document's text, as `application/json`

entry .construables   reads an API body of media type `application/json` as a `Json`; JVM only
  detail
      `jacinta.http`. The given which lets an API description (apoplexy's `Api`) construct a
      `Json` from a request or response whose media type is `application/json`.
  entry .jsonConstruable   `"application/json" is Construable to Json`

entry .optics   lenses and optics over `Json` documents, each a given to import by name
  detail
      `jacinta.optics`. These are what make `json.lens(…)` work: the panopticon lens for a
      named field, and the optics which reach into arrays. They live in a package rather than
      `Json`'s companion so that a missing import is a compile error rather than a silent
      change of behaviour; `import optics.*` brings them all (or `import soundness.optics.*`,
      where every library's optics merge). A lens assignment also needs dynamic access enabled
      and, to assign a plain value, `conversions.encodableToJson`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import dynamicAccess.dynamicJson, conversions.encodableToJson
      import optics.*

      case class Scene(number: Int, place: Text)
      case class Act(number: Int, scenes: List[Scene])
      case class Play(title: Text, opening: Act)

      val play = Play(t"Macbeth", Act(1, List(Scene(1, t"A heath")))).in[Json]
      play.lens(_.opening.number = 2.in[Json]).as[Play].opening.number   // 2
      play.lens(_.opening.scenes(Prim).place = t"Forres").as[Play]       // the first scene moved
      play.lens(_.opening.scenes(Each).place = t"x").as[Play]            // every scene moved
      play.lens(_.opening.scenes(Filter[Json](_.place.as[Text] == t"A heath")).place = t"x").as[Play]
      ```
  entry .jsonLens   a lens from a `Json` onto a named field, for `json.lens(_.name = …)`
      Needs dynamic access enabled (`dynamicAccess.dynamicJson`) and a `Tactic[Json.Error]`;
      reading a missing field yields an absent `Json`, and writing adds it.
  entry .jsonOrdinalOptical   an optic onto one array element by its `Ordinal`: `scenes(Prim)`
      Rebuilds the array immutably; an index past the end, or a non-array, leaves the
      document unchanged.
  entry .jsonEachOptical   the `Each` optic: applies a transform to every element of an array
  entry .jsonFilterOptical   the `Filter[Json](predicate)` optic: applies a transform to the elements matching it

entry .encodables   aviation's `Instant` and `Duration` encoded as milliseconds, each a given to import by name
  detail
      `jacinta.time`. An `Instant over Unix` encodes as its whole number of milliseconds since
      the epoch, and a `Duration` as its whole number of milliseconds.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import chronometries.unixChronometry
      import formatting.compactJsonFormatting
      import encodables.instantJsonEncodable

      Instant(1700000000000L).in[Json].show   // t"1700000000000"
      ```
  entry .instantJsonEncodable   `Instant over Unix` as a JSON integer of milliseconds
  entry .durationJsonEncodable   `Duration` as a JSON integer of milliseconds

entry .decodables   aviation's `Instant` and `Duration` decoded from milliseconds, through the tree
  detail
      `jacinta.time`. The tree-walking decoders; `parsables` are their direct-parsing twins.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import decodables.durationJsonDecodable

      t"5000".read[Json].as[Duration].value   // 5.0
      ```
  entry .instantJsonDecodable   `Instant over Unix` from a JSON integer of milliseconds
  entry .durationJsonDecodable   `Duration` from a JSON integer of milliseconds

entry .parsables   aviation's `Instant` and `Duration` read directly from the token stream
  detail
      `jacinta.time`. The direct-parsing counterparts of `decodables`: the number is read
      straight off the tokens, with no intermediate node, so a record with an `Instant` field
      parses directly when it has opted in.
  entry .instantJsonParsable   `Instant over Unix` read directly from a JSON integer of milliseconds
  entry .durationJsonParsable   `Duration` read directly from a JSON integer of milliseconds

entry .InlinableStager   the compile-time staging compiler behind `Inlinable.parsable`
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use. `jacinta.staged`. When a parser is generated for a record, a
      user-written `Inlinable` given for a field type is evaluated at expansion time in a
      fresh staging compiler over the macro classloader (with the current output directory
      excluded, since a class of the run in progress has no trustworthy classfile), so that
      the generator can call its `parse`. A type which cannot cross into the inner compiler —
      opaque, structural, or defined in the current run — degrades to a runtime seam.
      `Inlinable.Stager` loads `.Implementation` by name.
  entry .evaluate   evaluates the `Inlinable` instance for a type in a staging compiler, if it can
  entry .Implementation   the `Inlinable.Stager` which `Inlinable.parsable` loads by name
    detail
        Delegates `summon` to `.evaluate`.
    entry .summon   the `Inlinable` for a type, evaluated at expansion time, or `None`

entry .Inlinable   a compile-time code generator for one type's JSON parser and printer, composed into one flat parser
  detail
      `Inlinable` is the macro-time counterpart of `Json.Parsable` and `Json.Printable`: where
      a `Json.Parsable` instance *runs* against a `Json.Reader`, an `Inlinable` instance
      *generates* the code which would, as an `Expr`. `Inlinable.parsable[T]` composes the
      instances for `T` and every field type beneath it into a single monomorphic parser —
      typed locals for the fields, keys dispatched by comparing packed words against
      literals, nested records and collection loops inlined in place — with no runtime
      dispatch, no instance arrays and no adapter between composed parsers, and
      `Inlinable.printable[T]` does the same for writing. The result is spliced into the
      module which asked for it, so every field is read straight off the token stream and no
      `Json` tree is ever built.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import codepages.utf8Codepage
      import formatting.compactJsonFormatting

      case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)

      object Person:
        given Person is Json.Parsable = Inlinable.parsable[Person]
        given Person is Json.Printable = Inlinable.printable[Person]

      t"""{"name":"Hal","age":40}""".read[Person in Json]   // Person(t"Hal", 40)
      Person(t"Hal", 40).in[Text over Json]                  // t"""{"name":"Hal","age":40}"""
      ```

      The same two instances are generated once into a record's companion by `derives
      Json.Direct`, and `import codecs.direct` selects a generated parser and printer for
      every record in scope with nothing declared, so most code never calls `Inlinable`
      directly. What is generated for a type is decided by a resolution ladder, in order: a
      builtin leaf (`Int`, `Long`, `Double`, `Float`, `Boolean`, `Text`, `String`); a
      user-written `Inlinable` given, evaluated at expansion time; a structural generator for
      a case class (`.derived`) or an applied collection type — `List`, `Set`, `Sequence`,
      `Vector` and the stdlib's immutable collections, gathered element by element into the
      collection's builder; and otherwise the **runtime seam**, a spliced call to the field
      type's `Json.Parsable` or `Json.Field` (and, when printing, its `Encodable in Json`),
      which is how an `Optional[…]`, a `Map`, an enumeration or a sealed sum takes part. A
      recursive type — `Tree(children: List[Tree])` — ties the recursive occurrence back to
      the instance being generated, through its own nominal `Json.Parsable`, so the
      expansion terminates.

      Three facts govern a hand-written instance. First, an `Inlinable` given is an ordinary
      runtime value, so the generator can only call its `parse` by evaluating the given inside
      the macro, which needs an in-macro staging compiler: `jacinta.staged` supplies one
      (`Inlinable.Stager`), and without it on the compile classpath a field whose type has
      only a user-written instance takes the runtime seam. Second, the instance and its type
      must have been compiled in an *earlier* run than the expansion — a same-run instance has
      no trustworthy classfile and also degrades to the seam. Third, `Inlinable.parsable[T]`
      itself refuses a `T` with no generator of its own (a type whose only instance is a
      nominal `Json.Parsable`), with the error _jacinta: `T` has no generator of its own; use
      `Json.Parsable.staged` or `derived` rather than `Inlinable.parsable`_, since the result
      would either add nothing or call itself.

      An instance's `#prints` says whether it generates a printer; the default, `false`, keeps
      a parse-only instance honest, and a field whose instance does not print is written
      through its `Encodable in Json`. `#absent` is the code for a key which is missing from
      the object: by default it raises `Json.Error` through the tactic it is given, as the
      runtime instances do.
  refer jacinta.Json
  refer jacinta.Parser
  refer jacinta.inlining
  entry .parsable   `Inlinable.parsable[T]`: a monomorphic `Json.Parsable` for `T`, generated as the code compiles
      A macro. Resolves the `Inlinable` for `T` through the ladder described under
      `Inlinable` and splices one flat parser as a `Json.Parsable.Direct[T]`, which reads a
      value from the `Json.Reader`'s underlying `Parser` without building a `Json`. A type with
      no instance and no structural generator is a compile error, _jacinta: no Inlinable
      instance for `T`, and it is not an inlinable product; use `Json.Parsable.staged` or
      `derived`_. The usual home for the result is the type's companion:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import codepages.utf8Codepage

      case class Tree(label: Text, children: List[Tree])

      object Tree:
        given Tree is Json.Parsable = Inlinable.parsable[Tree]

      t"""{"label":"root","children":[{"label":"a","children":[]}]}""".read[Tree in Json]
      ```

      The semantics match `Json.Parsable.derived`: a declared default is used for a missing
      key, `@name` renames apply, unknown keys are skipped with `Parser#directSkipValue`,
      and an error focus names the field. Collections are gathered occurrence by occurrence
      into the collection's builder, with an index focus per element.
  entry .printable   `Inlinable.printable[T]`: a monomorphic `Json.Printable` for `T`, generated as the code compiles
      A macro: the writing counterpart of `.parsable`, a `Json.Printable.Direct[T]` which
      writes `T`'s compact JSON text straight into the writer, field by field, composed
      through the same instances. A type whose root instance does not print — a type which
      encodes as text, or whose only instance is a runtime `Encodable` — is a compile error,
      _jacinta: `T` has no generated printer; use its `Encodable in Json`_. A generated
      printer is used by `in[Text over Json]` and `in[Data over Json]` wherever the
      `Json.Formatting` in scope is compact; indented output still renders through the tree.
  entry .derived   the structural `Inlinable` for a case class: every field through its own instance
      `Inlinable.derived[T]` is the product generator as a value, for a case class `T`: it
      reflects `T` when its `parse` is invoked, so it needs no macro of its own and can be
      written as a given, `given Tree is Inlinable = Inlinable.derived`. It prints as well as
      parses.
  entry .ForJson   the carrier which lets a case class say `derives Inlinable.ForJson`
    detail
        A `Self`-typed typeclass has no type parameters, so `Inlinable` cannot appear in a
        `derives` clause. `case class Play(…) derives Inlinable.ForJson` synthesises a
        `ForJson[Play]` — which *is* a `Play is Inlinable`, delegating every method to
        `Inlinable.derived[Play]` — into `Play`'s companion, where the expansion-time summon
        finds it. The resolution ladder unwraps the delegate, so a structural instance keeps
        its generator identity.
    entry .derived   `ForJson.derived[T]`: wraps `Inlinable.derived[T]`, for the `derives` clause
    entry #parse   the delegate's generated parse
    entry #absent   the delegate's code for a missing key
    entry #prints   whether the delegate prints
    entry #write   the delegate's generated write
  entry .Generates   evidence that a type's codec is generated structurally, as a record
    detail
        `Inlinable.Generates[T]` is the condition on `import codecs.direct`'s generated
        instances: its given, `.generates`, is a macro which succeeds only for a case class the
        product generator supports and which does not encode as text, and fails — _jacinta:
        `T` has no generated codec_ — for everything else, so that the search for a generated
        instance fails and the derived path resolves instead. Not for direct use.
    entry .generates   the transparent inline given behind `Generates[T]`: a cheap shape test
  entry .Stager   the hook through which `jacinta.staged` evaluates a user-written instance at expansion time
    detail
        An `Inlinable` given is a runtime value, so the generator can only reach the code a
        user-written instance generates by running that given inside the macro, in a staging
        compiler. `jacinta.core` does not depend on the compiler jars, so the trait `Stager`
        declares the capability and `jacinta.staged` implements it (`InlinableStager`); the
        companion loads the implementation by its class name when `jacinta.staged` is on the
        macro classpath, and otherwise answers `None` for every type, which sends such fields
        to the runtime seam. A summon's result is cached for the expansion, so a type's
        staging summon runs at most once however often it recurs.
    entry .summon   the loaded stager's `summon[field]`, or `None` with no `jacinta.staged` on the classpath
    entry #summon   evaluates the `Inlinable` given for `field` live, inside the macro, if one resolves
  entry .int   the generator for an `Int` field: `reader.int()`, written as a bare number
  entry .long   the generator for a `Long` field: `reader.long()`, written as a bare number
  entry .double   the generator for a `Double` field: `reader.double()`, written as a bare number
  entry .float   the generator for a `Float` field: `reader.double().toFloat`; parse only
  entry .boolean   the generator for a `Boolean` field: `reader.boolean()`, written as `true` or `false`
  entry .text   the generator for a `Text` field: `reader.string()`, written as an escaped JSON string
  entry .string   the generator for a `String` field: `reader.string()`, written as an escaped JSON string
  entry .iterable   the generator for an applied collection whose element is `Inlinable`
      `collection[element] is Inlinable` for any `collection <: Iterable`, given an `element
      is Inlinable`: the generated loop opens the array with `Parser#directOpenArray`, steps
      with `Parser#directElementFirst` and `Parser#directElementNext`, parses each element
      inline and adds it to the collection's `Factory` builder. It prints if its element
      prints. Composed through ordinary given resolution when the instance graph is resolved
      inside the staging compiler.
  entry #parse   generates the code which reads a `Self` from a `Json.Reader`
      Takes the `Expr` of the reader and the `Quotes` and `Type[Self]` of the expansion, and
      returns the `Expr` of the decoded value, which the deriving macro splices in place. A
      local `def` or lambda in the generated code captures the exclusive parser read-only, so
      generated bodies bind the parser once and read through it directly.
  entry #prints   whether this instance also generates a printer; `false` unless overridden
  entry #write   generates the code which writes a `Self` into the JSON writer
      Takes the value and the writer — which travels as a neutral `AnyRef` carrier, since
      generated code is capture-erased, and is reasserted per use — and returns an
      `Expr[Unit]`. The default aborts the expansion with _jacinta: the Inlinable for `T`
      generates no printer_; an instance which overrides `#prints` to `true` overrides this
      too.
  entry #absent   generates the value for a key missing from the object
      The default is `Json.Parsable.missing[Self]()` through the tactic it is given, which
      raises `Json.Error`; an optional wrapper overrides it to produce its absent value.

entry .inlining   the machinery behind `Inlinable`: expansion-time resolution, structural generators and entry macros
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: the object holds the macro implementations `Inlinable.parsable`,
      `Inlinable.printable` and `Inlinable.Generates` expand to, the resolution ladder
      (builtin, staged summon, structural product or collection, runtime seam), the
      per-expansion cache which memoises staging summons and detects recursion, and the
      product and collection generators.
  entry .fieldSeam   the runtime seam: parses a field through its nominal `Json.Parsable` or `Json.Field`
      An inline method (a `summonFrom` may live only in one) which a generated parser
      splices for a field type with no `Inlinable`: a nominal `Json.Parsable` is called
      directly, and otherwise the field's `Json.Field`.
  entry .generates   the macro behind `Inlinable.Generates.generates`
  entry .inlinablePrintable   the macro behind `Inlinable.printable[T]`
  entry .inlinableParsable   the macro behind `Inlinable.parsable[T]`

entry .internal   the macro implementations behind `j"…"`, `jp"…"`, schema-typed navigation and `Json.Parsable.staged`
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: the object exists so that the macros have a stable home.
  entry .jsonPointer   the macro behind the `jp"…"` interpolator
      Decodes the literal as a `JsonPointer` while the code compiles, through the pointer's
      own `Decodable`, and maps a `JsonPointer.Error`'s offset back to the source so the
      compile error points at the offending character.
  entry .select   the macro behind `json.field` on a `Json`: checked against the schema type when there is one
      For a `Json of P from R` — a document `verify` has typed — the field is looked up in
      `P`'s structure and the result is `Json of <field type> from R`; a field `P` lacks is a
      compile error. For a plain `Json`, access is gated on the `dynamicAccess.dynamicJson`
      enabler and reads the field totally.
  entry .index   the macro behind `json(i)` on a `Json`: checked against the schema type when there is one
  entry .applied   the macro behind `json.field(i)` on a `Json`
  entry .interpolator   the macro behind the `j"…"` literal
      Parses the literal's parts, with a marker in place of each substitution, as the code
      compiles — so malformed JSON is a compile error at its position — and splices a
      `Json.Ast` with the substituted values filled in.
  entry .extractor   the macro behind `j"…"` as a pattern
      Compiles a JSON literal with `$name` holes into an extractor which matches a `Json`
      against it and binds the holes, as a `Boolean`, a single value or a tuple of them.
  entry .stagedParsable   the macro behind `Json.Parsable.staged` for a case class
      Generates a monomorphic parser: field values in typed locals, keys dispatched through a
      precomputed `Json.KeyTable` into an integer switch, builtin primitives read inline from
      the token stream, and the record built by a direct constructor call. Field types beyond
      the builtins go through `Json.Field` instances, so defaults, `@name`, absence and
      nullity, unknown keys, duplicates and error foci behave exactly as `Json.Parsable.derived`.
  entry .stagedSum   the macro behind `Json.Parsable.staged` for a sealed sum
      Locates the discriminator with a bounded scan-ahead which rewinds, dispatches by
      string comparison against the wire variant names, and parses the chosen variant
      through its `Json.Field`. A missing tag raises `Json.Error(Absent)` and an unknown tag
      wisteria's `Variant.Error`, as the derived engine does.

entry .jsonSchematics   constructors for a codec fused with the schema it reads or writes: `Decodable & Schematic`
  detail
      A `Json.Encodable` and a `Json.Decodable` each carry the `Morphology` of what they write
      or read, and `jsonSchematics.encodable[T]` and `jsonSchematics.decodable[T]` reify that
      shape into a `JsonSchema` and pair it with the codec in one instance, `T is Encodable &
      Schematic in Json over JsonSchema` (or `Decodable & Schematic`). The schema comes from
      the codec itself and is never derived separately, so the pair is coherent by
      construction: a type whose JSON codec routes through its `Encodable in Text` — an
      enumeration written as a string — gets a `JsonSchema.String`, where the standalone
      `Schematic` derivation would describe its structure as an object.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import codepages.utf8Codepage

      case class Contact(name: Text, age: Int, email: Text)

      val fused = jsonSchematics.decodable[Contact]
      fused.decoded(t"""{"name": "Percy", "age": 30, "email": "percy@example.com"}""".read[Json])
      fused.schema()   // a JsonSchema.Object with the three properties
      ```

      These are methods, not givens, on purpose: a `Decodable & Schematic` given is a subtype
      of both its codec typeclass and `Schematic`, so as a given it would be ambiguous for
      any bare `Schematic` summon. Call one where a fused instance is wanted, or bind it as a
      local given: `given (Contact is Decodable & Schematic in Json over JsonSchema) =
      jsonSchematics.decodable[Contact]`.
  refer jacinta.JsonSchema
  entry .encodable   the `Json.Encodable` for `T` fused with the `JsonSchema` of what it writes
      Needs a `T is Json.Encodable` in scope; `encoded` delegates to it, and `schema()` is
      `JsonSchema.reify` of its `shape()`.
  entry .decodable   the `Json.Decodable` for `T` fused with the `JsonSchema` of what it reads
      Needs a `T is Json.Decodable` in scope; `decoded` delegates to it, and `schema()` is
      `JsonSchema.reify` of its `shape()`.

entry .JsonSchema   a JSON Schema as a value: derived from a Scala type, read from a document, written as one
  detail
      `JsonSchema` is an enum whose cases are the schema node kinds — `Object`, `Array`,
      `String`, `Number`, `Integer`, `Boolean`, `Null` and `Ref` — each carrying the keywords
      JSON Schema allows for it, an optional `description`, and an `optional` flag which
      records that the node admits `null` or absence (a `"null"` among the `type`s, OpenAPI's
      `nullable`, or an `Optional` field in a derived schema). It is the `Schematic`
      transport for JSON: `T is Schematic over JsonSchema` gives a schema for `T`, the
      companion derives one for any case class or sealed hierarchy (`JsonSchema.derived[T]`,
      from wisteria's `Derivable`, and the inline given `.schematic` which resolves it for any
      reflectable type), and a `JsonSchema` encodes to and decodes from a standard JSON Schema
      document.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import codepages.utf8Codepage

      case class Contact(name: Text, age: Int, email: Optional[Text])

      JsonSchema.derived[Contact].schema() match
        case schema: JsonSchema.Object => schema.required   // List(t"name", t"age")
        case _                         => Unset

      infer[List[Int] is Schematic over JsonSchema].schema()   // Array(items = Integer())
      (JsonSchema.Integer(): JsonSchema).in[Json].show          // contains "type":"integer"
      t"""{"type": ["string", "null"]}""".read[Json].as[JsonSchema]   // String(optional = true)
      ```

      The cases, each a case class with defaults for every parameter:

      - `Object(description, properties: Map[Text, JsonSchema], optional, required:
        Optional[List[Text]], enum: Optional[List[Json]], additionalProperties: Boolean,
        oneOf, additionalSchema: Optional[JsonSchema], allOf, anyOf, not, const)`. A derived
        schema's `required` lists the fields which are not `Optional`; a sealed hierarchy
        derives as an `Object` whose `oneOf` holds one schema per variant, with `kind`
        required. `additionalProperties` is `true` when the schema admits further properties,
        whether by a bare `true` or by a schema for them, which `additionalSchema` then
        carries; a boolean schema `true` reads as `Object(additionalProperties = true)`.
      - `Array(description, items: Optional[JsonSchema], minItems, maxItems, optional,
        maxContains, minContains)`: a `List` or `Set` derives as an `Array` of its element's
        schema.
      - `String(description, minLength, maxLength, pattern: Optional[Text], format:
        Optional[JsonSchema.Format], optional)`: `Text` and `EmailAddress` derive as a
        `String`.
      - `Number(description, multipleOf, maximum, minimum, exclusiveMinimum, exclusiveMaximum,
        optional)`, all bounds `Optional[Double]`: `Float` and `Double` derive as a `Number`.
      - `Integer(description, maximum, minimum, exclusiveMinimum, exclusiveMaximum, optional,
        format)`, all bounds `Optional[Long]`: `Byte`, `Short`, `Int` and `Long` derive as an
        `Integer`.
      - `Boolean(description, optional)` and `Null(description, optional)`.
      - `Ref(pointer: JsonPointer, description, optional)`: a `{"$ref": "#/…"}` reference,
        kept unresolved so a cyclic schema graph stays finite; `verify` leaves a `Ref` node
        unchecked.

      The schema cases `Array`, `String`, `Number`, `Boolean` shadow the prelude's names
      inside `object JsonSchema` only; from outside, `JsonSchema.Array(…)` and the prelude's
      `Array` coexist. A field's description in a derived schema comes from the `@memo`
      annotation on the field, and a variant's from `@memo` on the variant.
  refer jacinta.memo
  refer jacinta.verify
  refer jacinta.jsonSchematics
  refer jacinta.Json
  entry .byteSchematic   `Byte` derives as `Integer()`
  entry .shortSchematic   `Short` derives as `Integer()`
  entry .intSchematic   `Int` derives as `Integer()`
  entry .longSchematic   `Long` derives as `Integer()`
  entry .floatSchematic   `Float` derives as `Number()`
  entry .doubleSchematic   `Double` derives as `Number()`
  entry .textSchematic   `Text` derives as `String()`
  entry .emailSchematic   `EmailAddress` derives as `String()`
      The schema records no `format`: the fused `jsonSchematics` instances and `Json.Provider`
      are where `format: "email"` is interpreted.
  entry .booleanSchematic   `Boolean` derives as `Boolean()`
  entry .optionalSchematic   an `Optional[T]` (or any `Mandatable` wrapper) derives as `T`'s schema marked optional
      Takes the schema of the inner type and sets its `optional` flag through `.optional`, so
      a derived `Object` omits the field from `required`.
  entry .listSchematic   a `List[T]` derives as `Array(items = T's schema)`
  entry .setSchematic   a `Set[T]` derives as `Array(items = T's schema)`
  entry .mapSchematic   a `Map[K, V]` with a text-encodable key derives as `Object(additionalProperties = true)`
      The value schema is not recorded: JSON Schema's `additionalProperties` keyword could
      carry it, and does when a schema is read from a document (`additionalSchema`), but the
      derivation does not yet write it.
  entry .schematic   the inline given which derives a schema for any reflectable type
      `[value: Reflection] => value is Schematic over JsonSchema`, expanding to `derived`: a
      case class becomes an `Object` with a property per field and the non-optional fields
      `required`; a sealed hierarchy becomes an `Object` with a `oneOf` per variant. Each
      field's and variant's `@memo` annotations become its `description`, joined with
      newlines when there are several.
  entry .jsonSchemaSchematic   `JsonSchema` itself derives as a permissive `Object()`
      A manual instance, found in preference to `.schematic`, so that deriving a schema for a
      type which has a `JsonSchema` field terminates instead of recursing through the schema
      of schemas forever.
  entry .pointerEncodable   `JsonPointer` encodes as its text, so a `Ref` serialises as a string
      Provided explicitly as a `Json.Encodable` so generic derivation treats a pointer as a
      leaf rather than deriving its path structure.
  entry .encodable   writes a `JsonSchema` as a JSON Schema document
      The `Json.Encodable` for `JsonSchema`: each case becomes an object with its `type`
      (`"object"`, `"array"`, `"string"`, …) and its keywords, through the `type`-discriminated
      derivation; a `Ref` becomes a bare `{"$ref": "…"}`. Being a `Json.Encodable` rather
      than a plain codec is what lets the collection and map codecs resolve a nested
      `JsonSchema` to this instance instead of deriving a diverging schema of schemas.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val schema: JsonSchema = JsonSchema.Object(properties = Map(t"a" -> JsonSchema.String()))
      schema.in[Json].show   // {"type":"object","properties":{"a":{"type":"string"}},…}
      ```
  entry .decodable   reads a `JsonSchema` from a JSON Schema document
      The `Json.Decodable` for `JsonSchema`, hand-written because a schema node is
      discriminated by the *value* of its `type` field, which may be a name, a list of names
      (draft 2019-09 and OpenAPI 3.1), or absent for a `$ref` or an untyped object. It needs a
      `Tactic[Json.Error]` and a `Tactic[JsonPointer.Error]` (for a malformed `$ref`), and the
      instance captures both, so a summon which names its type must allow the capture,
      `summon[(JsonSchema is Json.Decodable)^]`; `as[JsonSchema]` needs nothing special.

      What it accepts: a boolean schema (`true` is `Object(additionalProperties = true)`,
      `false` admits nothing); a `"null"` among the `type`s or OpenAPI 3.0's `nullable: true`,
      recorded as `optional`; draft 4's `exclusiveMinimum: true` qualifying `minimum` as well
      as the later numeric form, both reading to the same model; `additionalProperties` as a
      boolean or as a schema; a `format` of any name, unknown ones kept as
      `JsonSchema.Format.Other`; and a node with no recognised `type` read as an `Object`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import codepages.utf8Codepage

      t"""{"type": "number", "minimum": 3, "exclusiveMinimum": true}""".read[Json].as[JsonSchema]
      // JsonSchema.Number(exclusiveMinimum = 3.0)
      ```
  entry .discriminatedUnion   how a `JsonSchema` document says which case it is: by its `type` field
      The `Discriminable` instance the derived encoder uses: the discriminator is the
      lowercased `type` keyword (`"object"`, `"integer"`, …), read back capitalised to the
      case name. It is what makes `{"type": "integer"}` encode without a `kind` wrapper.
  entry .reify   a format-neutral `Morphology` as a `JsonSchema`
      The bridge from a codec's declared shape to a schema:

      ```tabular
      column  Morphology
      column  JSON Schema
      row  `Str`  `String()`
      row  `Whole`  `Integer()`
      row  `Real`  `Number()`
      row  `Bool`  `Boolean()`
      row  `Empty`  `Null()`
      row  `Any`, `Dict`  `Object(additionalProperties = true)`
      row  `Opt(inner)`  the inner schema, marked optional
      row  `Arr(items)`  an `Array`
      row  `Obj(fields, required)`  an `Object` with those properties
      row  `OneOf(variants)`  an `Object` whose `oneOf` lists them, with `kind` required
      ```

      `verify` and `jsonSchematics` are built on it, which is how `jacinta.core` stays free of
      any schema dependency.
  entry .optional   the same schema with its `optional` flag set
      Copies any case with `optional = true`; `.optionalSchematic` and the codecs for
      `Optional` and `Option` fields use it.
  entry .conjunction   the derivation of a case class's schema: an `Object` of its fields
      The `Derivable` hook: a property per field through the field type's `Schematic`, the
      fields whose schema is not optional listed as `required`, and each field's `@memo`
      descriptions joined as its `description`. Not called directly; `.schematic` and
      `derived` reach it.
  entry .disjunction   the derivation of a sealed hierarchy's schema: an `Object` with a `oneOf` per variant
      The `Derivable` hook: one schema per variant, with the variant's `@memo` as its
      description, under `oneOf`, with `kind` required — the discriminator field the
      `jsonByKindDiscriminable` encoding writes. Not called directly.
  entry .Format   the `format` keyword of a string or integer schema: an open vocabulary with the known names as cases
    detail
        The cases are JSON Schema's own formats — `DateTime`, `Date`, `Time`, `Duration`,
        `Email`, `Hostname`, `Ipv4`, `Ipv6`, `Uri`, `UriReference`, `UriTemplate`, `Uuid`,
        `JsonPointer`, `RelativeJsonPointer`, `Regex` — and OpenAPI's additions `Int32`,
        `Int64`, `Float`, `Double`, `Byte`, `Binary` and `Password`; anything else a document
        invents (`snowflake`, `unix-time`) is `Other(name)`, so decoding is total. A
        `Json.Provider` reads a `format` as the type it names, `email` as an email address,
        `uuid` as a `Uuid`, and so on.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        JsonSchema.Format.DateTime.encode             // t"date-time"
        t"date-time".as[JsonSchema.Format]            // JsonSchema.Format.DateTime
        t"snowflake".as[JsonSchema.Format]            // JsonSchema.Format.Other(t"snowflake")
        ```
    entry .encodable   writes a format as its kebab-case name: `DateTime` as `date-time`, `Other(name)` as `name`
    entry .decodable   reads a format from its name; an unknown name is `Other(name)`, never a failure
  entry #optional   whether the node admits `null` or absence
      Set for an `Optional` field in a derived schema, a `"null"` among a document's `type`s
      or its `nullable: true`; a derived `Object` omits an optional property from `required`,
      and `verify` accepts an absent value only where the schema is optional.
  entry #description   the node's `description`, or `Unset`
  entry #description_=   the same node with a description: `schema.description = t"…"`
      Copies any case with the description replaced; `@memo` sets it during derivation.

entry .memo   an annotation which gives a field or a variant a `description` in the derived schema
  detail
      `@memo(t"…")` on a case class field, or on a variant of a sealed hierarchy, becomes the
      `description` of that property or alternative in the `JsonSchema` the companion
      derives. Several `@memo`s on one field are joined with newlines.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      case class Marked(@memo(t"the count") n: Int)

      JsonSchema.derived[Marked].schema() match
        case schema: JsonSchema.Object => schema.properties(t"n").let(_.description)
        case _                         => Unset
      // t"the count"
      ```
  refer jacinta.JsonSchema
  entry #description   the text of the annotation

entry .verify   checks a document against a type's schema and re-types it, so its fields can be navigated with compile-time checking
    An extension on `Json`. `json.verify[T]` reifies the schema `T`'s `Json.Decodable`
    declares (through `JsonSchema.reify` of its `shape()`), walks the document against it,
    and on success returns the same document typed `Json of T from T`: a `Json` whose phantom
    `Topic` is the position within the schema and whose `Origin` is the root. On the first
    mismatch it raises `Json.Error` — `Reason.NotType` for a value of the wrong kind,
    `Reason.Absent` for a required field the document lacks — so it needs a
    `Tactic[Json.Error]`, and `safely(json.verify[T])` is `Unset` for a nonconformant
    document.

    ```scala fuel
    frame
      label A required field is missing: verification fails
      code
          import soundness.*
          import strategies.throwUnsafely
          import codepages.utf8Codepage

          case class Contact(name: Text, age: Int, email: Text)

          val hal = t"""{"name": "Hal"}""".read[Json]
          safely(hal.verify[Contact]).absent   // true: `age` and `email` are absent
    frame
      label A field of the wrong kind still fails
      code
          import soundness.*
          import strategies.throwUnsafely
          import codepages.utf8Codepage

          case class Contact(name: Text, age: Int, email: Text)

          val hal = t"""{"name": "Hal", "age": "old", "email": "hal@example.com"}""".read[Json]
          safely(hal.verify[Contact]).absent   // true: `age` is a string, not a number
    frame
      label A conformant document verifies, decodes and navigates with checked field names
      code
          import soundness.*
          import strategies.throwUnsafely
          import codepages.utf8Codepage

          case class Contact(name: Text, age: Int, email: Text)

          val hal = t"""{"name": "Hal", "age": 41, "email": "hal@example.com"}""".read[Json]
          hal.verify[Contact].as[Contact]       // Contact(t"Hal", 41, t"hal@example.com")
          hal.verify[Contact].name.as[Text]     // t"Hal"
          hal.verify[Contact].nope              // does not compile
    ##
    ```

    The verified document's type is what licenses the navigation: `verified.name`,
    `verified.workplace.city` and `verified.members(1).name` are each checked against the
    schema's structure as the code compiles — a field the type lacks, an index into a field
    which is not an array, or a field of a scalar is a compile error naming the problem — and
    no `dynamicAccess.dynamicJson` import is needed. A plain, unverified `Json` still
    requires the enabler, and the error says so, so the two kinds of access cannot be
    confused.

    What the check covers: each property the schema names is checked recursively, and may be
    absent only where its schema is optional; properties the schema does not name are
    allowed; an array's every element is checked against `items`; a `oneOf` passes if any
    variant does; and a `Ref` node is left unchecked, since a reference cannot be resolved
    without the root document. A derived `Optional[T]` field may be absent, but a JSON `null` in
    its place is not an absence and fails the check against the inner schema.

entry .Parser   the streaming JSON tokenizer behind every `read[Json]`: one exclusive, pooled instance per thread
  detail
      `Parser` is the single-pass JSON parser which every `read[Json]`, `Json.Ast.parse`,
      `j"…"` literal and direct `read[T in Json]` goes through. It is a stateful, exclusive
      capability (`caps.ExclusiveCapability, caps.Stateful`) over a `zephyrine.Cursor` of
      `Data`, so every method which moves through the input is an `update def`, callable only
      through an exclusive `Parser^` reference; one instance lives in a per-thread pool and is
      reset for each parse, so parsing allocates no parser and keeps its scratch buffers and
      its key cache warm from one document to the next.

      Ordinary code never names it. The user-facing entry points are `read[Json]` (and
      `read[Json]` over a `Stream[Data]` or an `Iterator[Data]`, which parses block by block
      without first joining the input), `Json.Ast.parse`, and the `Json.Parsable` instances
      which read a value straight off the token stream through a `Json.Reader`:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import codepages.utf8Codepage

      case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)
      given Person is Json.Parsable = Json.Parsable.derived

      t"""{"name": "Hal", "age": 40}""".read[Json]
      t"""{"name": "Hal", "age": 40}""".read[Person in Json]
      ```

      The class is public as a *type* because the parsers `Inlinable.parsable[T]` and
      `Json.Parsable.staged` generate are spliced into the user's own module and read through
      its direct rim (`#directString`, `#directLong`, `#directElementFirst`, …), which is a few
      percent faster than going through `Json.Reader`'s per-token forwarders. A hand-written
      `Json.Parsable` should use `Json.Reader` instead: the reader carries its own
      `Tactic[Parse.Error]`, and the only way to reach the parser behind a live read is sealed
      inside jacinta. A `new Parser` is possible but not the intended route — it has no pool,
      and `#resetData` must be called before `#parse`.

      What it produces is `Json.Ast`: a `String`, a `Long`, a `Double`, a `Bcd` (for numbers
      beyond fifteen digits, as the `NumberMode` in scope says), `true`/`false`,
      `Json.JsonNull`, an `Array[Any]` for an object or a mixed array, and a packed
      `Array[Int]` or `Array[Long]` for an array of nothing but small numbers, which decodes
      to the same values as the boxed form but costs one allocation. Object keys of up to
      sixteen ASCII bytes are interned through a per-thread cache, so a record shape repeated
      across documents reuses its key strings.

      Numbers which fit a `Long` or a `Double` exactly are decoded on a fast path; longer or
      more precise ones are kept as `Bcd` digits so nothing is lost, unless
      `numberModes.doubleNumberMode` or `numberModes.bcdNumberMode` is imported to trade that
      precision for throughput. A leading UTF-8 byte order mark is skipped. Strings are decoded
      from UTF-8, with every escape honoured, and an input which is not valid JSON aborts with
      a `Parse.Error` whose `Json.Ast.Issue` names the fault — `EmptyInput`,
      `SpuriousContent` after the value, `ExpectedColon`, `NumberHasLeadingZero`,
      `IncorrectEscape`, `PrematureEnd`, and so on — at its byte position. Under
      `parsing.trackPositions` the same parse also builds a `Json.PositionIndex`, so
      `locate` can map a node back to its line and column.

      Performance: on the documents of the benchmark corpus the streaming parse runs within
      about fifteen percent of Jackson, and direct reads through a generated `Json.Parsable`
      skip the tree altogether.
  refer jacinta.Json
  refer jacinta.Inlinable
  refer jacinta.NumberMode
  entry #Region   a marked position in the input, `Cursor.Mark`, as the parser's own alias
      A `Region` is an absolute position in the input, taken when a token begins and used to
      slice the token's bytes once it ends or to report an error's range. It is `Cursor.Mark`
      from zephyrine, and is only meaningful inside the hold which produced it.
  entry #resetData   points the parser at a whole document held in memory
      Rebuilds the cursor over `input: Data` and clears every piece of per-parse state: the
      string buffer, the scratch arrays and the position index. The pool's entry points call
      this before each `#parse`; a hand-held parser must do the same.
  entry #resetIterator   points the parser at a document arriving as blocks of bytes
      As `#resetData`, over an `Iterator[Data]` which the parser consumes (the parameter is
      `consume`d, so the caller gives the iterator up). Blocks are refilled as the parse
      advances, and a token which straddles two blocks — a number, a string, a keyword — is
      made contiguous by the cursor, so the result is identical to parsing the joined bytes.
  entry #resetStream   points the parser at a `Stream[Data] over Credit`
      As `#resetIterator`, over a credit-metered `Stream[Data]`: how `read[Json]` from a
      socket or a file parses as the bytes arrive, in bounded memory, rather than after
      joining the whole input.
  entry #parse   parses one complete JSON document and returns its `Json.Ast`
      Skips a byte order mark and leading whitespace, parses exactly one value, and then
      requires only whitespace to the end of the input: anything else aborts with
      `Json.Ast.Issue.SpuriousContent`, and an empty input with `Json.Ast.Issue.EmptyInput`.
      It needs a `Tactic[Parse.Error]`, through which every malformed-input fault is raised
      with its byte position. When the parser is tracking positions, the root
      `Json.PositionIndex` is built alongside the tree.
  entry #directSkipValue   consumes one whole value without building anything
      The direct rim's skip: a validated scan over one value — scalar, array or object, to any
      depth — which enforces the same grammar and raises the same `Json.Ast.Issue`s as
      `#parse`, but allocates no node, no buffer and no key string. A generated parser uses
      it for every key it does not recognise, so an unknown field costs only the bytes it
      occupies.
  entry #directString   consumes one JSON string token and returns it as a `String`
      Skips whitespace, then requires a `"`; anything else aborts with
      `Json.Ast.Issue.ExpectedString`. A plain-ASCII string which closes inside the current
      buffer is copied straight out; one with escapes or non-ASCII bytes, or one which
      crosses a block boundary, is decoded by the general path, with the same result.
  entry #directBoolean   consumes `true` or `false`
      Skips whitespace, then reads the keyword, aborting with `Json.Ast.Issue.ExpectedTrue`
      or `ExpectedFalse` if the bytes are not exactly the keyword.
  entry #directLong   consumes one integer token as a `Long`
      Skips whitespace, then reads up to eighteen digits with an optional minus sign on a
      fast path which never refills the buffer. A fraction, an exponent, a nineteenth digit,
      a leading zero (which must be rejected, as `NumberHasLeadingZero`) or a number touching
      the window's end falls back to the general number parser, so the value is the same
      whichever path ran. `Json.Reader#long` and `Json.Reader#int` are this call.
  entry #directDouble   consumes one number token as a `Double`
      Skips whitespace, then reads a number with up to fifteen significant digits and an
      exponent within the range where mantissa and power of ten are both exact, so the fast
      path is bit-identical to the general path; anything else — a sixteenth digit, a leading
      zero before a digit, a large exponent, the window's end — takes the general path.
      `Json.Reader#double` is this call.
  entry #directOpenObject   consumes `{` and enters an object
      Skips whitespace, then requires `{` (else `Json.Ast.Issue.ExpectedObject`) and pushes
      a nesting level, whose separator state the key-stepping methods maintain so that a
      missing or doubled comma is rejected exactly as `#parse` would reject it.
  entry #directKeyWordFirst   the first key of an object, packed into a `Long`, or a sentinel
      For generated parsers which know statically that they are reading the first key of an
      object: expects no separating comma. A key of one to sixteen printable ASCII bytes with
      no escapes is returned packed, its first eight bytes in the result and the rest in
      `#directKeyWordHigh`, so a generated parser can dispatch by comparing against literal
      constants without materialising the key. Two values are impossible as packed keys and
      serve as sentinels: `-1` after consuming the closing `}` (`Json.Reader.KeyEnd`), and
      `-2` (`Json.Reader.KeyOpaque`) when the fast path cannot decide here — the key is
      escaped, longer than sixteen bytes, empty, at the window's edge or malformed — in which
      case the caller resolves it through the general, interned-name step
      (`Json.Reader.keyNameOf`).
  entry #directKeyWordNext   a subsequent key of an object, packed into a `Long`, or a sentinel
      As `#directKeyWordFirst`, for every key after the first: expects a `,` before the key
      (or the closing `}`), and consults no per-key state. The same sentinels apply.
  entry #directKeyWordHigh   bytes nine to sixteen of the key most recently packed
      The high word of the key `#directKeyWordFirst` or `#directKeyWordNext` just scanned,
      zero for a key of eight bytes or fewer. Read it immediately after the low word, before
      the next key is scanned.
  entry #directOpenArray   consumes `[` and enters an array
      Skips whitespace, then requires `[` (else `Json.Ast.Issue.ExpectedArray`) and pushes a
      nesting level for the element-stepping methods.
  entry #directElement   steps to the next element of the open array, or closes it
      Returns `true` positioned at the next element, with any separating comma consumed, and
      `false` after consuming the closing `]`. It tracks whether the current level has yielded
      an element yet, so a missing comma between elements, or a comma before the first, is
      rejected as `#parse` would reject it.
  entry #directElementFirst   the first step of an array loop: an element or the closing `]`
      As `#directElement`, for a loop which knows statically that this is its first step:
      expects a value or `]`, never a comma, and marks the level as begun. A generated
      collection parser pairs it with `#directElementNext`.
  entry #directElementNext   a later step of an array loop: `,` then an element, or the closing `]`
      As `#directElement`, after the first element: expects `,` or `]`, and consults no
      per-element state. A `,` followed by `]` aborts with
      `Json.Ast.Issue.ExpectedSomeValue`.
